window.WIKI_SEARCH = [{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Introduction A practical guide to modding Ballistic.exe with UE4SS and Lua. This wiki covers setting up your mod folder, finding game objects, hooking functions and troubleshooting scripts. Start with a small change, check it in-game, and build from there. You don’t need Unreal Editor for the Lua examples here. UE4SS loads your scripts alongside the game and lets them interact with objects and functions that Unreal exposes at runtime. Before you start The examples come from the Windows demo. Game updates can change class names, functions and file layouts. Keep a backup of your saves and check your own build’s log when something behaves differently. Getting started If you’re new to Unreal modding, follow these pages in order. Guide What you’ll do Installing UE4SS Set up the loader and check that it starts. Your first mod Create a Lua script and find its output in the log. Working with hooks Run code when a game function is called. Debugging Track down missing objects, Lua errors and crashes. What can you do with Lua? Lua mods can inspect loaded objects, call reflected functions, respond to game events and create UI using assets the game already contains. Some changes need only a few lines of code. Adjust workshop behaviour and add controls to existing menus. Read weapon or target information for tools and overlays. Spawn and arrange existing assets at runtime. Add keybinds, diagnostics and small quality-of-life changes. These are starting points, not a guarantee that every game system is accessible. See Custom content for the limits of a Lua-only mod. A small example A mod begins with Scripts/main.lua . This is enough to check that the loader is running your file: print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hello from Ballistic!\\n\") The message appears in ue4ss/UE4SS.log after you launch the game. The first mod guide shows the complete folder structure. Using this wiki Use the sidebar to browse, or press Ctrl + K to search the text of every guide. Each article has links to its sections and a copy button beside code examples. For the complete loader API, use the UE4SS documentation . The game-specific paths here are observations from the demo, rather than a supported game SDK. Choose a learning path You can follow the wiki in order, but you do not need to understand every game system before writing a small mod. Pick the path that matches the change you want to make, and stop at a result you can actually verify. I have not written a Lua mod Install the loader and confirm a fresh log. Run a single startup message before calling any game function. Work through Lua basics until tables, callbacks and nil checks make sense. Split the mod into modules only once the single-file version works. Build the read-only weapon inspector and compare its snapshots. Your first milestone is not an elaborate menu. It is a script that you can install, identify in a log, trigger once and disable cleanly. I want to change a weapon or attachment Read Weapon assemblies before editing a mesh or part. Then follow Attachments & rails and Weapon stats . The workflow is: identify the actual live gun, record a baseline, use one normal attachment action, inspect the changed branch and verify the result after leaving the menu. Save/reload is a separate milestone. I want to add a menu control Start with Finding game objects and Workshop UI . Learn which widget owns the canvas and how it is rebuilt. Create a visible but inactive control first, confirm its placement, then connect exactly one action. Test closing the menu while that action is pending. I want targets, scoring or a range feature Follow target discovery , hit attribution and range travel . Keep a cosmetic scene, a scoring mode and a cooked map as separate goals. A new card and a row name do not produce a new map package. Read an example’s context before copying it A block labelled “inside the callback above” is a fragment, not a complete main.lua. A block marked “pure Lua” does not need a live Unreal object. A block marked “mutating” changes state and should not be used as a discovery probe. When a result is shown as \u003craw numeric value> , it describes an output format rather than a captured measurement. Exact numeric expectations appear only where a specific recorded fixture supports them. Evidence levels explain the difference. When you get stuck Reduce the problem to the last result you could prove. Did the file load? Was the class loaded? Did the callback run? Was it the active instance? Did the normal game action complete? Those are different questions. Use Debugging to work through them instead of adding more code around an unknown failure. All guides Getting started Introduction Installing UE4SS Your first mod Lua basics for modding Organising a mod Changelog Lua scripting Working with hooks Finding game objects Timers & cleanup Reading data tables Debugging Performance & profiling Walkthroughs Build a weapon inspector Test a mod step by step Weapons Weapon assemblies Attachments & rails Weapon stats Shots & ballistics Ranges & targets Finding & tracking targets Hits & scoring Range menus & travel Menus & saves Workshop UI Ownership & unlocks Reading & backing up saves Reference Multiplayer architecture Game paths & classes Custom content Packaging a Lua mod Checking game updates Coverage & testing notes Tools & resources"},{"id":"installation","title":"Installing UE4SS","text":"Installing UE4SS Set up the loader, then check the log before adding a mod. What you need The Windows version of Ballistic.exe. A UE4SS release compatible with your game build. A text editor, such as Visual Studio Code. The local demo tests used UE4SS 3.0.1 Beta #0 , commit 5e627997 . This records a tested setup; it is not a recommendation to overwrite a working installation with a different release. Downloads: UE4SS releases · Experimental builds . Find the game folder In Steam, open the game’s settings and choose Manage → Browse local files . From there, open: Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ You should see Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe . The smaller Ballistic.exe in the game’s top-level folder is a launcher. Install the loader beside the shipping executable. Install the loader Close the game. Extract the chosen UE4SS package beside the shipping executable, preserving the package’s folder layout. Merge folders carefully if you already have other mods installed. Launch the game once, then close it and inspect the log. The setup used for these guides has the following layout. Other loader packages can use a different working directory; follow the installation instructions for your release . Win64/ ├── Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe ├── dwmapi.dll └── ue4ss/ ├── UE4SS.dll ├── UE4SS-settings.ini ├── UE4SS.log └── Mods/ Check the log Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Look for the loader version and startup messages. The file should have a fresh timestamp from your last launch. If it is missing or unchanged, check the extraction location and that the files match your loader release. If the game crashes before mods load, resolve the loader setup before adding scripts. Next step Continue to Your first mod to create a script and verify it with a unique log message. Step-by-step installation check 1. Preserve a working setup If mods already work, record the current loader version from the log and copy its configuration before replacing anything. Keep the existing Mods directory separate from downloaded loader files. A new loader is not automatically the answer to a broken script. Use the release archive’s contents as the authority for its layout. Some packages keep UE4SS.dll in a ue4ss subfolder; others use a different root/working-directory arrangement. Do not move individual DLLs into a layout guessed from an unrelated tutorial. 2. Check the executable you are beside Walk from the Steam install folder through Ballistic → Binaries → Win64. Confirm the filename is Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe. If the directory only contains the small top-level launcher, go deeper before extracting. This distinction matters because a proxy loader must be discovered by the process it is intended to load into. A correctly named file beside the wrong executable can leave you with no loader startup and no useful Lua error. 3. Extract without adding an extra directory After extraction, inspect the result rather than trusting the extraction dialog. A common mistake is an extra archive-name folder between Win64 and the loader files, or between Mods and the mod itself. Compare your final paths with the package’s instructions. Do not rename a dependency to “fix” a path problem. Preserve filenames and move back to a known layout if the first launch fails. 4. Do a loader-only launch Start the game before adding your test script. Wait for a normal menu, exit, and inspect a log with a fresh timestamp. Record the loader’s version and whether startup reached its normal event loop. This creates a baseline for distinguishing an installation problem from a later mod problem. Observed result Interpretation Next action No fresh loader log The loader may not have started, or you are reading the wrong working directory. Recheck the shipping directory and package layout. Fresh log, no test-mod prefix The loader started; your mod discovery or script load still needs checking. Follow the first-mod folder check. Lua syntax error naming your file The script was found but could not compile. Fix the indicated source before engine debugging. Crash before your script loads The first failure is not proof your callback ran. Compare against the loader-only baseline. 5. Add one test mod Use the minimal first-mod layout , launch again and look for its unique prefix. Keep the loader configuration unchanged during this step. If you change loader version, file layout and script behaviour together, a successful or failed launch is harder to explain. Upgrading and removing Close the game before updating. Identify files owned by the loader and files owned by individual mods, then follow the chosen release’s update instructions. Preserve your mod configuration and remove obsolete loader files only when you know they belong to that old installation. To disable one example mod, remove its enable marker with the game closed. Do not delete shared loader files to disable a single script. Uninstalling a behaviour mod may leave changes already saved by the game, so refit or restore a disposable assembly before testing removal. For version-specific details, consult the loader installation guide . This wiki’s local setup records are not a compatibility list for every release."},{"id":"first-mod","title":"Your first mod","text":"Your first mod Create a mod folder, write one Lua file, and check that it runs. Create the folder Inside your loader’s Mods folder, create MyFirstMod . Add a Scripts folder and an empty file named enabled.txt . ue4ss/Mods/ └── MyFirstMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Enable file extensions in Windows Explorer so the script isn’t accidentally saved as main.lua.txt . Write the script Open main.lua in a text editor and add: local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. message .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Loaded successfully.\") Use a distinct prefix in every log message. It makes your output easy to find when several mods are enabled. Run the mod Save the file. Fully close and restart the game. Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Search for [MyFirstMod] Loaded successfully. If the message appears, your script is loading. If it doesn’t, check the folder names and the log’s Lua errors. Add a keybind This example logs a message when you press F9. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod. RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] F9 pressed.\\n\") end Keep engine work on the game thread A log message is a small first test. If your keybind changes Unreal objects, schedule that work with ExecuteInGameThread . See Finding game objects . Disable or share the mod With the game closed, remove the mod’s enabled.txt to disable it. For a small Lua-only release, package your mod folder, scripts and enable marker with clear installation instructions. Don’t bundle the game, saves, logs or your entire UE4SS installation. Walkthrough: prove each stage separately 1. Check the real filenames In Explorer, enable file-name extensions. Create main.lua as a text file and check the final extension after saving. A file named main.lua.txt can look correct when extensions are hidden, but it is not the expected entry script. The enable marker belongs next to Scripts, not inside it. The Scripts directory belongs inside your mod folder. Verify those three relationships before changing Lua code. 2. Understand when the file runs Top-level Lua statements execute when the loader starts the mod. A function definition creates a function; it does not execute its body until called. RegisterKeyBind stores a callback that will run later. Put the startup message outside that callback so you can distinguish “file loaded” from “key pressed”. 3. Add a counter to the key callback local presses = 0 RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() presses = presses + 1 print(string.format(\"[MyFirstMod] Press count: %d\\n\", presses)) end) Restart and press once, then again. Expect counts one and two. If each press prints two messages, check for duplicate registrations or a second enabled copy of the mod before blaming input timing. The counter is in-memory; restarting resets it. 4. Introduce a game-thread boundary RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] Game-thread callback reached.\\n\") end) end) This still only logs. It teaches where future engine calls belong without introducing object lookup at the same time. Execution is scheduled, so don’t assume the result exists synchronously on the line after ExecuteInGameThread returns. 5. Test disabling Close the game, remove the enable marker and launch again. Your startup prefix and key response should be absent. If they remain, find the running copy by its startup version/path and check for another installed folder. Do not delete arbitrary files while the process is active. What to build next Read Lua basics if the syntax is unfamiliar. Use Organising a mod to add a configuration module. Then follow Build a weapon inspector for a complete read-only feature instead of a context-dependent fragment. Your completion check for this page is small and concrete: one startup line, one increment per chosen input, no callback when disabled, and a log from the current process. Nothing here proves that a weapon or widget is ready yet."},{"id":"lua-basics","title":"Lua basics for modding","text":"Lua basics for modding Understand the language before adding Unreal objects to the mix. Before you start Finish Your first mod so you have a script whose log message you can find. Use a disposable test mod for the snippets here. Copy one example at a time, restart, and compare its output with the explanation before combining examples. The examples use Lua, as loaded by UE4SS. Luau is a related language with different features; don’t paste Roblox services, Luau type annotations or Roblox instance APIs into a UE4SS script and expect them to exist. 1. Values and local variables local enabled = true local sampleLimit = 12 local label = \"My mod\" local currentWeapon = nil print(label .. \": \" .. tostring(sampleLimit) .. \"\\n\") local limits a variable to its surrounding scope. true and false are booleans; 12 is a number; quoted text is a string; nil means no value. Two dots join strings. Use tostring when a value might be a number, boolean or nil. Lua treats only false and nil as false in a condition. The number 0 and an empty string are both truthy. If a setting must be a boolean, validate its type instead of assuming that zero disables it. 2. Conditions and early returns local function describe(value) if value == nil then return \"No value\" end if type(value) ~= \"number\" then return \"Not a number\" end if value \u003c 0 then return \"Negative number\" end return \"Zero or positive number\" end print(describe(nil) .. \"\\n\") print(describe(-2) .. \"\\n\") Expected output is No value , then Negative number . == compares values; = assigns a value; ~= means not equal. Each if needs then and end . Early returns make object checks easier to read because the main operation does not sit inside several nested conditions. 3. Functions and callbacks A function is a value you can call or hand to another function. A callback is a function handed to something that runs it later. Do not call the callback while registering it. local function onPressed() print(\"[MyMod] Key callback ran.\\n\") end RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed) -- Do not write RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed()). The final comment shows a common mistake: the parentheses execute onPressed immediately, then pass its result to RegisterKeyBind. Without the parentheses, the function itself is registered. Choose a key not already used by another mod. 4. Tables and loops local names = {\"barrel\", \"rail\", \"grip\"} for i, name in ipairs(names) do print(i .. \": \" .. name .. \"\\n\") end local settings = {Enabled = true, MaxParts = 12} print(tostring(settings.MaxParts) .. \"\\n\") Ordinary Lua list indices conventionally start at one. ipairs walks a dense sequence until the first nil. A keyed table is useful for settings or named state. settings.MaxParts is shorthand for settings[\"MaxParts\"] . These rules describe Lua tables. A UE4SS wrapper for an Unreal array, set or map is a different object with its own supported operations. Don’t use a table example as proof that an engine container supports the same iterator. See Arrays and containers . 5. Dot versus colon local counter = {value = 0} function counter:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end counter:add(3) -- Equivalent ordinary Lua call: counter.add(counter, 3) print(counter.value .. \"\\n\") A colon call supplies the receiver as the first argument. Use the form expected by the API you’re calling. object:IsValid() and module.create() are not interchangeable conventions. In engine bindings, an incorrectly called function can fail before your intended operation happens. 6. Errors and type checks A syntax error prevents the chunk from loading. A runtime error occurs while executing a loaded chunk. If a variable is nil, ask why it never received a value rather than adding a default that hides the missing object. pcall returns a success boolean followed by the result or error. It catches Lua errors, not every native access violation. assert is useful for rejecting an invalid configuration immediately; it is less helpful as a replacement for waiting for an expected menu to load. Practice before engine work Change the sample limit and predict the output. Pass a string to describe and confirm the type branch. Make a list of three labels and print each once. Put the counter in its own module, following Organising a mod . Only then introduce a real weapon or widget reference. If a plain Lua example fails, solve that first. Adding Unreal state makes a language mistake harder to isolate."},{"id":"project-structure","title":"Organising a mod","text":"Organising a mod Keep startup, settings, engine access and feature state in separate places. 1. Decide what each file owns For a small feature, four files are often enough. This is a practical starting point rather than a rule that every function needs its own module. File Responsibility main.lua Validate startup settings and register callbacks once. config.lua Return defaults a user can edit with the game closed. log.lua Provide a consistent message prefix. inspect.lua Read engine state and return a result. The weapon inspector walkthrough uses exactly this layout. For a larger mod, group related modules under folders such as ui/ and gameplay/ rather than growing main.lua into every subsystem. 2. Return a module -- Scripts/counter.lua local Counter = {} function Counter.create() local state = {value = 0} function state:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end return state end return Counter local Counter = require(\"counter\") local first = Counter.create() local second = Counter.create() first:add(2) print(first.value .. \", \" .. second.value .. \"\\n\") The output should be 2, 0 . Returning a constructor lets two features have separate state without copying module code. Keep the module’s public operations small; callers shouldn’t need to reach into every internal field. 3. Validate settings before registering work local Config = require(\"config\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be a boolean\") assert(type(Config.MaxParts) == \"number\", \"MaxParts must be a number\") assert(Config.MaxParts % 1 == 0, \"MaxParts must be an integer\") assert(Config.MaxParts >= 1 and Config.MaxParts \u003c= 64, \"MaxParts must be from 1 to 64\") Explain each setting in the installation guide and pick bounded defaults. A typo should produce a useful log message, not an enormous actor loop or an unexplained half-initialised feature. Do not store live actor or widget objects in a user configuration file. A persisted preference can survive travel; a world-owned object usually cannot. 4. Keep dependencies one-way Have main.lua import the feature module and supply configuration. Avoid a feature importing main.lua to ask for a value while main.lua is still loading that feature. Pass a callback or state object instead. require normally caches a loaded module within its Lua environment. Editing a module on disk does not prove that a running instance has reloaded it. Use the full restart workflow that was validated for your loader, and log a version string to identify the code actually running. 5. Separate three kinds of state Configuration: user choices, limits and feature switches. Session state: a custom round, network epoch or queued action. World state: controllers, actors, widgets and cached components. A single “reset everything” function can erase preferences unnecessarily. Reset world caches on travel, cancel actions when their context changes, and reset round scores only when the round flow calls for it. 6. Keep names portable Use consistent filename casing and relative module names. Avoid a personal absolute install path inside a shared script. Keep mod-owned data under a deliberate data directory, not the current working directory by accident. If you change a module’s name, update its requires and the release archive’s explicit file list together. Test an upgrade from the old release: a removed module left on disk can mask a packaging mistake. Check the refactor Confirm the same startup message appears once. Trigger the feature and compare behaviour with the previous version. Use an invalid setting and check the diagnostic. Disable the feature and confirm no action callback is registered. Restart after removing an obsolete module to confirm nothing secretly depends on it."},{"id":"changelog","title":"Changelog","text":"Changelog New guides, corrections and changes to the wiki. Newest updates come first. These entries track the documentation and website, not changes to Ballistic.exe or individual mods. Next update Added Five detailed guides: Lua basics for modding , Organising a mod , Performance & profiling , Build a weapon inspector and Test a mod step by step . A downloadable read-only WorkshopInspector teaching example, with every Lua file shown and explained in its walkthrough. Numbered exercises, expected results, parameter explanations, diagnostic tables, lifecycle checks and acceptance tests throughout the existing 25 guides. A repeatable testing workflow that distinguishes plain Lua checks, native fixtures, actual game use and real two-PC sessions. Changed Moved Changelog into Getting started in the sidebar and guide index. Expanded the setup, scripting and game-system articles from short overviews into practical step-by-step references. Explained native versus Blueprint hook phases and linked the version-specific API reference. Added detailed investigations for attachment removal, stat baselines, projectile attribution, target bodies, UI rebuilds, range arrival, save snapshots and ownership filtering. The build now syncs the inspector’s displayed code with its source files and rebuilds its archive from an explicit file list. Updated navigation, search, the complete guide index, README and contributor guidance for 30 guides plus the changelog. Fixed Rebuilt the no-JavaScript navigation with consistent separators and links to every page. The contents list now tracks the reading position on long pages, including the last section at the bottom of an article. 7 November 2026 Added The first nine guides: introduction, UE4SS installation, a first Lua mod, hooks, objects, debugging, game paths, custom content and tools. Sixteen more guides covering timers and cleanup, data tables, weapon assemblies, attachments and rails, weapon stats, shot events, targets, scoring, workshop UI, ranges, saves, ownership, multiplayer architecture, packaging, update checks and coverage. A grouped index of all guides and a coverage page explaining the limits of the current documentation. Full-text search, keyboard shortcuts, navigation filtering, section links and code-copy buttons. Dark mode by default, a saved theme choice, mobile navigation and print styles. A contributor guide, pull request template and a manual GitHub Pages workflow. This changelog, with a place to record future additions and fixes. Changed Organised the site into separate HTML guides with shared CSS and JavaScript. Moved page titles, order and sidebar groups into one shared navigation file. Grouped the expanded sidebar by topic and opened the current section automatically. Added checks for page listings, local links and section anchors before building the site. Rewrote the README as short setup and editing instructions, with badges for guide count and estimated coverage. Fixed Long reference tables now scroll inside the article on phones instead of squeezing the text into narrow columns. The README coverage badge now opens the coverage page. Search can be closed with Escape, and the header search label stays on one line. Corrected Windows path handling in the local preview and build tools."},{"id":"hooks","title":"Working with hooks","text":"Working with hooks A hook runs your code when the game calls an Unreal function. Find a function Use the UE4SS object viewer or an object dump to locate the exact reflected function path. Class names and function names are case-sensitive. A Blueprint function often has a path like this: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive This path was observed in the demo’s attachment UI. It may not be loaded until workshop assets are present. Wait for the function Don’t assume a Blueprint is available when main.lua first runs. Check for it on the game thread, and register your hook once. local path = \"/Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive\" local installed = false LoopAsync(2000, function() if installed then return end ExecuteInGameThread(function() if installed then return end local fn = StaticFindObject(path) if not fn or not fn:IsValid() then return end RegisterHook(path, function(context) local object = context:get() if not object or not object:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] Workshop lookup called.\\n\") end) installed = true print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hook installed.\\n\") end) end Read parameters carefully Hook arguments can be UE4SS parameter wrappers. Use the access pattern documented for the parameter’s type, and inspect the signature before reading or changing it. Callback phase and return-parameter behaviour can differ between native and Blueprint functions. The sample above only reads the context and logs a call. It does not alter mount compatibility or attachments. Avoid common mistakes Don’t install the same hook on every timer tick. Don’t call the hooked function from its own callback unless you have a deliberate recursion guard. Don’t assume a path from an older game build is still correct. Don’t use a Lua error handler as protection against native memory crashes. Check the UE4SS API documentation for your loader version before writing to parameters or relying on pre/post callbacks. Walkthrough: investigate one hook 1. Find the owner and full signature Search the object dump for the function name, then read the owning class and parameter properties around it. Keep native paths beginning with /Script separate from Blueprint paths beginning with /Game. A similarly named function on a derived class can be a different integration point. Record parameter order and types. A bool, object, struct, out array and return value require different handling. Don’t infer the whole signature from one callback that happened not to crash. 2. Wait for the function to exist A cooked Blueprint can be present in the archive but absent from the current object registry. Trigger the normal menu or range that loads it, then retry StaticFindObject at a low frequency. Register once after the lookup is valid. Do not run LoadAsset repeatedly from a background callback just to force progress. RegisterHook targets reflected UFunctions. The current API reference does not support delegate functions through that registration path; a widget delegate name is not automatically a hookable action. 3. Choose the correct callback phase The current RegisterHook reference distinguishes native and Blueprint paths: the second argument is a pre callback for /Script functions and an after callback for other paths; an optional third argument supplies a native post callback. Match the docs to your installed loader rather than naming every second-argument callback a “pre hook”. Start with a callback that logs and returns nothing. A hook return value can change a function’s result; accidentally returning a helper’s value can therefore change gameplay. 4. Read context before parameters local function onCalled(context) local actor = context:get() if not actor or not actor:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Called on \" .. actor:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Confirm this runs on the expected instance. Only then add one parameter at a time. Copy numeric values or component coordinates into ordinary Lua tables if they need to outlive the callback; don’t assume a parameter wrapper remains a safe reference after the call finishes. 5. Keep the hook identifiers local preId, postId = RegisterHook(path, onCalled) local function removeMyHook() if preId == nil then return end UnregisterHook(path, preId, postId) preId, postId = nil, nil end Keep both returned IDs with the path. This is your hook’s identity; don’t try to unregister another feature’s callback by guessing its IDs. For a reload/reinitialisation path, remove the old registration before installing the replacement where supported. Recursion and reentrancy A hooked function can call related functions that eventually reach the same hook again. Calling it yourself from its callback can create direct recursion. Use a deliberate guard around the mutating operation, release it on Lua failure, and avoid treating every nested call as a separate user action. For UI hooks, a function may run during both construction and refresh. Adding a button each time without checking its existing parent creates duplicates. The fix belongs in your construction lifecycle, not in an arbitrary delay. When a UI return parameter is missing In the recorded attachment popup test, the Blueprint post callback did not supply the return parameter expected by the first tab implementation. Reading the widget’s stored AttachmentsList property supplied the needed list instead. That is a specific observed workaround, not a universal rule that return parameters never work. Dump the signature, log what the callback actually supplies and verify the resulting visible list. A registered hook with zero useful callbacks is not a completed feature."},{"id":"objects","title":"Finding game objects","text":"Finding game objects Find an object, check that it is valid, then use it while it still belongs to the current world. Loaded objects StaticFindObject looks up an object that is already loaded. It does not automatically load every asset from the game archive. ExecuteInGameThread(function() local object = StaticFindObject( \"/Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data\" ) if not object or not object:IsValid() then print(\"[MyFirstMod] Attachment table is not loaded yet.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Instances and defaults A class describes an object type. A class default object contains its default values. A live actor or widget is an instance. Editing a default is not the same as changing the weapon or menu the player is currently using. Read the full object name and confirm which object you found before writing to it. World changes Travelling from the bunker to a range replaces many actors and widgets. A reference that worked before travel can become invalid afterwards. Reacquire world-specific objects after travel. Check validity immediately before use. Clear cached widget references when the menu is destroyed. Keep track of actors your mod spawns so you can clean them up. Arrays and containers Unreal containers exposed through UE4SS are not always ordinary Lua tables. In this local loader, using ipairs on some Unreal arrays continued beyond the reported length. A bounded loop worked for those arrays: local points = editor.POIs local count = #points assert(count \u003c= 512, \"Unexpected attachment-point count\") for i = 1, count do local point = points[i] if point and point:IsValid() then print(point:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end This example requires a valid editor instance. Set and map containers have different APIs; don’t apply the array pattern to every container. Reflected methods Use methods present in the dump for your build. For actor transforms, the local bindings exposed K2_GetActorLocation and K2_GetActorRotation . Calling an unavailable method produced a TrivialObject error rather than a normal Lua function call. Walkthrough: find the right instance 1. Identify what kind of name you have An asset package path, a generated class path, an instance full name and a short class name are different identifiers. For example, a Blueprint-generated class can end in _C ; an actor instance can add a runtime suffix. Don’t strip or append suffixes blindly to turn one into another. Lookup Use it for Do not assume StaticFindObject An exact object already loaded under that name. That it reads unloaded archive content. LoadAsset Request a known cooked asset on the game thread. That a table label is a valid asset path. FindAllOf Inspect loaded instances by the expected short class name. That the first result is the active menu or player. 2. Establish current-world context Start with a valid viewport, get its world and use that world to resolve GameplayStatics context. Log the world full name and compare it after travel. A globally loaded class can be reused across worlds; its live actors and widgets cannot be treated the same way. For a weapon query, decide whether you mean the range pawn’s equipped gun or the workshop’s displayed gun. Both can be valid objects while only one is relevant to the action. 3. Filter candidates deliberately If a global query returns several editors, inspect validity, visibility, parent/owner and the referenced current weapon. Log candidate names before selecting one. “First valid” is a useful discovery shortcut but a weak production rule if old or hidden widgets remain loaded. The downloadable inspector reports visible editor candidates explicitly. It doesn’t claim that visibility uniquely proves input focus. 4. Copy values you need later local raw = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() local position = {X = raw.X, Y = raw.Y, Z = raw.Z} -- position is plain Lua data for a comparison or message. -- Reacquire actor before a later engine operation. A copied position is not a live binding. It stays unchanged if the actor moves. That makes it useful as a baseline, but you must capture another sample to detect movement. 5. Guard the container and its length Confirm whether an output is a plain Lua table, a parameter wrapper or an Unreal container. Use a bounded iteration count, handle a missing result, and avoid continuing to use an array whose owner is being rebuilt by the operation you just performed. A bounded log is useful for discovery. For a production operation, silently truncating a larger array can be a correctness bug; decide whether to reject the operation, continue in batches or report the limit. Expected result and failure checks You should be able to name the world, the live instance and why you selected it. After travel, the old cached reference should be discarded and the new instance discovered. If the field is unavailable, check the actual owning class and its reflection signature instead of treating the unavailable value as zero. If a method call reports TrivialObject, inspect the dump for a reflected alternative. A C++ method name visible in an engine tutorial is not proof that your Lua binding exposes it."},{"id":"runtime","title":"Timers & cleanup","text":"Timers & cleanup Make a small mod behave properly after menu changes, travel and repeated clicks. Pick a timing source Use a slow discovery loop to wait for assets, a game-thread callback for engine work, and a game clock for elapsed-time calculations. They solve different problems. Task Approach Wait for a widget class A bounded or low-frequency discovery loop. Read or change Unreal objects Run the operation on the game thread. Animate an overlay Use elapsed time; don’t tie speed to callback count. Expire a notification Compare timestamps rather than assuming an exact timer interval. Avoid overlapping work A background loop can queue a new callback before the previous one has run. Keep one pending callback and clear the flag even after a Lua error: local queued = false LoopAsync(1000, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Read or update the current world's objects here. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end Cancel stale actions For a queued attachment operation, capture the weapon identity and an operation number when the user clicks. Before every delayed step, check that the same editor is open, the weapon is unchanged and the operation number still matches. Stop when any check fails. Setting a Lua boolean doesn’t cancel a callback already in the queue. The callback needs to check that boolean before doing engine work. Separate world state from settings Keep preferences such as overlay size separate from actor references. On world change, clear actor/widget caches, pending shot IDs and world-specific timers. Rebuild them from the new world. An object name alone can be reused after travel; a world identity plus object identity is a better cache key. Clean up what you create Track only the actors and widgets your mod creates. Remove its widgets from their parents and destroy its actors while they are still valid in that world. Don’t run a global actor sweep and delete everything with a similar class name. If you temporarily change an existing actor’s visibility, collision or physics, remember its original values and restore those values when your feature closes. Walkthrough: make a cancellable job Use this pattern for work spread over multiple callbacks, such as clearing an attachment list one point at a time. The example below is pure Lua state; it does not remove attachments. local generation = 0 local current = nil local function begin(contextId) generation = generation + 1 current = {id = generation, context = contextId, cancelled = false} return current end local function stillCurrent(job, contextId) return current == job and not job.cancelled and job.id == generation and job.context == contextId end local function cancel() generation = generation + 1 if current then current.cancelled = true end current = nil end 1. Capture the context when starting Use the current world and weapon/menu identity to construct contextId. If an action is already pending, reject a second click or cancel the old action explicitly. Do not queue several identical destructive jobs because the button still looks enabled. 2. Validate inside the delayed callback A check made before scheduling only proves the context was valid at scheduling time. Check stillCurrent inside the callback, then reacquire the editor and weapon. If the user left the menu or switched guns, stop before touching an attachment. ExecuteWithDelay schedules asynchronous work in the local API used here. The engine operation should still be dispatched onto the game thread. The nominal delay is not a promise that the callback runs at an exact instant. 3. Perform one bounded operation Process one point or a small fixed batch. If that changes the menu’s point list, return and reacquire on the next step. Mark a target done only when the intended action is accepted; otherwise keep a bounded retry policy and a visible failure result. 4. Finish every path Success, cancellation and Lua failure should all release the pending state and update the UI. A “Clearing…” button that never re-enables after an error is a lifecycle bug even if the underlying removal succeeded. If the feature owns a hook, widget or actor, give that object a specific teardown path. Merely dropping the Lua variable does not guarantee that an engine delegate or panel stops referencing it. Clock and unit checklist Write milliseconds or seconds in the variable name when it helps. ExecuteWithDelay’s integer delay and GetRealTimeSeconds do not use the same unit. Use a single clock for an elapsed-time calculation; don’t subtract an os.time wall-clock value from a world-relative game clock. When a world clock restarts, reset any next-update deadline and interpolation history using it. A negative time delta can otherwise keep an update gated for an unexpectedly long time. Acceptance test Start the job and confirm only one pending operation exists. Click again and confirm your documented duplicate-click behaviour. Change context before the next step and confirm no further action occurs. Force a Lua failure in a disposable test callback and confirm the busy state clears. Travel and confirm the old job cannot act on the new world. What was checked Queued-work guards and per-world cleanup are used in the local UI, target and attachment fixtures. This guide is a lifecycle pattern, not a universal timer-cancellation API. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"data-tables","title":"Reading data tables","text":"Reading data tables Find the table, inspect a row, and keep discovery separate from mutation. Find a table The demo has tables for attachment mount definitions and range menu data. You can inspect them once the asset is loaded: ExecuteInGameThread(function() local data = LoadAsset(\"/Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData\") if not data or not data:IsValid() then return end local names = data:GetRowNames() for i = 1, #names do print(\"[MyMod] Range row: \" .. tostring(names[i]) .. \"\\n\") end end Read a row In the tested loader, FindRow accepted a Lua string as the row name. Passing an FName in that particular call returned no row. This is different from APIs that explicitly expect an FName . local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") if row then local map = row.MapName_3_02B8F3864CDF2B67468FDD80A1766519 print(\"[MyMod] Map: \" .. map:ToString() .. \"\\n\") end The long suffix belongs to a Blueprint struct field. Copy the field name from your build’s dump instead of shortening it to MapName and assuming that will work. Useful tables Asset What it describes /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData Range rows, display information and destination names. /Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data Attachment mount relationships. The attachment table contained 86 mount rows in the inspected build. That is a build observation, not a constant your mod should require forever. Watch the field types A row can contain names, text, class references and soft class references. A soft reference is not the same thing as a loaded class. Converting, loading or iterating them can trigger additional engine work. Keep an initial probe small: list names, read one known row, then inspect one field. Don’t start by copying the entire table into Lua. Before changing data Changing a row affects every consumer that reads the live table. Rewriting recursive mount relationships can also introduce cycles. A previous broad attachment-row copying experiment froze the game; a targeted lookup hook was used instead. Make any edits reversible, preserve original values and test teardown. Adding a menu row doesn’t create its map or asset. See Range menus & travel . Walkthrough: document one table field 1. List names without changing rows Run the row-name example in a read-only test and save a bounded log. Confirm the table is valid and the result count is plausible. Treat the count as a discovery result; a hardcoded 86-row assertion is appropriate only for a fixture tied to that exact build, not for every future installation. 2. Pick one known row Use a row the normal game menu already displays. In the local demo, PistolRange was useful because the menu and actual pool destination were known. Record the exact string and whether FindRow accepts the representation you passed. 3. Read the schema next to the field A Blueprint-generated struct’s display label can differ from its reflected property identifier. Look for the full generated field name and property type in the dump. In a range row, a MapName field is a name; a Displayname field is text. Both can be converted for logging, but that does not make their original types interchangeable. 4. Compare the row with the UI Verify that the row’s label appears where expected and that selecting it reaches the recorded package. If the card is absent, inspect unlock filtering and menu construction. If the card is visible but travel fails, inspect destination availability instead. Those failures occur at different layers. 5. Record a useful finding Table asset: Game build: Row name and argument type: Field identifier and reflected type: Read value: Normal UI action used to verify it: What was not tested: This is more useful than publishing a large unannotated dump. It tells another modder which representation worked and which visible behaviour confirmed the interpretation. Copy plain values, not the entire row For a menu list, extract only the ordinary fields your feature needs, such as a display string and a verified destination ID. Keep soft-reference loading out of a broad initial dump. An accessor that is safe on one name field does not prove that every nested soft class array can be copied safely. If you mutate a row, keep the original values and a precise restoration path. Never assume that disabling your mod automatically rolls back a live table change or data already persisted by the game. What was checked Row-name listing and string FindRow calls were exercised locally. Broad soft-reference copying is a known failed experiment, not a recommended technique. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"debugging","title":"Debugging","text":"Debugging Start with the log and a repeatable set of steps. Read the log In the layout used here, the loader log is Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Check its timestamp and search for your mod’s prefix. Log when your script loads, when a hook installs and when the relevant callback runs. Those are three different checks. local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Script loaded.\") Common errors Symptom What to check No log message Mod folder, enabled.txt, script filename and extraction location. Object is invalid Whether the asset is loaded and whether the world or menu changed. Hook never runs Exact function path, loaded class and whether the game uses that event. TrivialObject is not callable Whether the method is reflected in this game build. Freeze while iterating Container type, loop bounds and recursive hook calls. Native access violation The full crash stack, last callback and recently accessed engine objects. Handle Lua errors A protected call can log a Lua failure and stop that operation: local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Run one operation you are investigating here. end) if not ok then print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end pcall does not make an invalid native pointer safe. If the process crashes inside the engine or loader, simplify the callback and inspect the native stack. Reproduce a problem Write down the game build, loader version and mod version. Use a minimal setup with the mods needed for the test. Record the exact weapon, part or menu involved. Compare the result before and after the operation. Restart fully, then repeat the same steps. For a stat problem, record the value before fitting a part, after fitting it, and after removing it. “It went negative” doesn’t show whether the value dropped below its original baseline. After a game update Check function paths and signatures again. Confirm asset availability, menu construction and save behaviour separately. A successful startup log only proves that startup completed. Walkthrough: locate the first broken layer 1. Is the code you edited actually loaded? Add a unique startup version message, fully restart, and check the log timestamp. If an old version still appears, identify the installed copy before editing more files. A source folder and a packaged archive can both exist without either being the one the loader runs. 2. Does the relevant callback run? Log the first call, the context’s full name and a counter. If there are zero calls, inspect the path, class loading and the real UI flow. If there are too many calls, inspect nested recursion and construction/refresh events. Rate-limit repeated diagnostics after the first few samples. 3. Is the data what you think it is? Log the Lua type, reflected field type and an ordinary copied value. A non-nil wrapper is not automatically a loaded UObject. A property returning a placeholder is not automatically a callable function. Distinguish unavailable from a real zero, false or empty result. 4. Does the smallest native action work? Use a disposable fixture if the action can change an assembly or save. Call one known function with one known compatible argument. Confirm the actual engine state afterwards. A successful pcall means no Lua error was reported; it does not establish the visible result or absence of a later native crash. 5. Does the UI reflect the result? Check the model, label or menu after the action and after closing/reopening it. If the engine state changed but the display did not, investigate the refresh path. Avoid rewriting the state a second time simply because the first redraw was delayed. 6. Does the result survive the intended lifecycle? Travel, return, restart and load a disposable save only when persistence is part of the feature. If the behaviour works until travel, focus on stale references and world resets. If it fails only after restart, focus on the persisted representation and reconstruction path. Reading a Lua traceback Start with the first error message and the file/line where your code failed. Follow the call stack outward to learn which callback entered it. A later cleanup error may be secondary; keep the original failure visible rather than replacing it with another generic message. Use xpcall with debug.traceback when a small diagnostic operation needs a complete Lua stack. Do not wrap an entire feature in a silent pcall and continue as though it succeeded. local ok, result = xpcall(function() return inspectCurrentContext() end, debug.traceback) if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] Inspection failed: \" .. tostring(result) .. \"\\n\") return end Reading a native crash report Keep the exception address, module names and the last relevant fresh log lines. A UE4SS frame in a stack identifies where execution passed, not automatically which mod caused the invalid state. Compare a loader-only run, a minimal feature run and the exact reproduction. If the fault only appears after several menu rebuilds, test object lifetime and delegate ownership. If it appears during container membership checks, check reference representation and the binding’s expected types. Do not “fix” a native fault by adding a Lua error handler that cannot intercept it. A good final report State the smallest reproduction, observed values, versions, test method and remaining uncertainty. Attach a relevant excerpt rather than a complete private save or an enormous log. The testing walkthrough provides a template and an acceptance matrix."},{"id":"performance","title":"Performance & profiling","text":"Performance & profiling Measure the expensive boundary between Lua and the engine before changing update rates. 1. Make a baseline Record the game scene, mod configuration and action sequence. Compare the same range and weapon with the feature disabled and enabled. A different target layout or gun assembly can change workload enough to invalidate the comparison. Separate idle cost from action cost. A UI might be cheap while closed but hitch when rebuilding every widget after a click. An actor registry might be cheap to update but expensive to rediscover. 2. Count work before timing it local counters = {discoveries = 0, samples = 0, setters = 0} local function report() print(string.format( \"[MyMod] discoveries=%d samples=%d setters=%d\\n\", counters.discoveries, counters.samples, counters.setters )) end For an unchanged menu, how many labels are set again each second? For a static target, how often do you reload its mesh? A counter often exposes redundant work before a profiler is needed. Report periodically, not on every frame. Logging can become part of the hitch you are trying to diagnose. 3. Give tasks different cadences Work Reasonable starting approach Find loaded classes Slow retry until ready; stop once installed. Rediscover a target registry Occasional refresh plus world-change invalidation. Update known moving actors Bounded frequent samples. Render interpolated visuals A local visual cadence independent of network sends. Capture weapon meshes On identity changes, with a slower periodic verification. These are design starting points, not universal tick rates. Measure your scene and adjust the budget. Reducing cosmetic updates must not silently reduce gameplay-event reliability. 4. Avoid catch-up bursts A stalled frame can leave several nominal deadlines behind. For a cosmetic update, replaying all missed steps in one frame makes the stall worse. Advance from elapsed time and cap work instead. local nextUpdate = nil local lastNow = nil local function shouldUpdate(now, rate) local period = 1 / rate if not nextUpdate or not lastNow or now \u003c lastNow then nextUpdate = now end lastNow = now if now \u003c nextUpdate then return false end nextUpdate = math.max(nextUpdate + period, now + period * 0.1) return true end Call this with one coherent clock. The backwards-clock branch matters after a world reset when a world-relative clock starts over. A setting change should explicitly reset the schedule too, rather than waiting on a deadline calculated for the old rate. 5. Reuse unchanged engine objects Cache asset references after successful loading. For a weapon proxy, compare the mesh identity before replacing an actor. For a menu, compare the intended text, enabled state and visibility with the last successful values before issuing setters. Only mark a cached value as applied after the setter succeeds. If a native operation fails, caching the desired value too early can prevent the retry that would restore the UI. 6. Budget large registries Process a fixed number of ordinary targets per update and carry a cursor forward. Prioritise moving or recently hit targets separately, with its own cap. Without the second cap, “priority” can grow into another unbounded full-world pass. Keep event queues bounded and define backpressure. Silently dropping a cosmetic sample is different from dropping an acknowledged hit or save-transfer chunk. Verify the improvement Repeat the baseline sequence, compare counters, and check that the feature still works after travel, a missing object and a setter failure. Faster code that displays stale state is not a successful optimisation."},{"id":"workshop-inspector","title":"Build a weapon inspector","text":"Build a weapon inspector A complete, read-only example that connects settings, keybinds, game-thread work and weapon inspection. What you will build Press one key to print a snapshot to UE4SS.log: the current world, the equipped weapon or visible workbench-editor candidates, selected raw stats and a bounded list of child actors. The example contains no attachment setter, score award, save/apply operation or travel action. Download the WorkshopInspector example . You can also open the individual source files below. This is teaching code adapted from the recorded local demo patterns, not a guarantee for every game or UE4SS build. Before you start A working UE4SS installation and a known log location. The first mod startup check completed. An unused key; the example defaults to F9. A workbench attachment editor or a gun equipped in a range. Close the game while creating or editing files. Keep this folder separate from other mods; do not merge its scripts into another mod’s Scripts folder. 1. Create the layout ue4ss/Mods/WorkshopInspector/ ├── enabled.txt ├── README.md └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua ├── log.lua └── inspect.lua The archive uses a mod-folder root. Extract it into ue4ss/Mods , not into Scripts and not into a second WorkshopInspector directory. Check that the final path ends in WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua . 2. Add configuration return { Enabled = true, KeyName = \"F9\", MaxParts = 12, MaxEditors = 8 } Open config.lua . Enabled is a boolean. KeyName names an entry in the loader’s Key table. MaxParts limits printed child names; it does not remove parts or limit the actual assembly. MaxEditors bounds the number of loaded editor candidates inspected. These limits are deliberately small so a key press does not produce an unbounded log dump. Change them with the game closed and restart to load the new values. 3. Add a logger local Log = {} function Log.info(message) print(\"[WorkshopInspector] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end return Log Open log.lua . One function gives every message the same prefix. tostring makes diagnostics tolerant of nil and non-string values. The newline is the Lua escape \\n , not an actual line break inside the quoted source string. 4. Read engine state local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") local Inspect = {} local function valid(object) if object == nil then return false end local ok, result = pcall(function() return object:IsValid() end) return ok and result == true end local function object(value) if valid(value) then return value end local ok, result = pcall(function() return value:get() end) if ok and valid(result) then return result end end local function name(value) if not valid(value) then return \"\u003cunavailable>\" end return value:GetFullName() or \"\u003cunnamed>\" end function Inspect.capture(config) local lines = {} local function add(text) lines[#lines + 1] = text end local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not valid(viewport) then return {\"Viewport is not ready.\"} end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not valid(world) then return {\"World is not ready.\"} end add(\"World: \" .. name(world)) local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") if not valid(gameplay) then return {\"GameplayStatics is unavailable.\"} end local pc = gameplay:GetPlayerController(world, 0) if not valid(pc) then add(\"Local controller is not ready.\") return lines end local function describe(gun, label) if not valid(gun) then return end add(label .. \": \" .. name(gun)) for _, field in ipairs({\"TotalErgo\", \"TotalInstalledErgo\", \"TotalRecoilControlModifier\"}) do local ok, value = pcall(function() return gun[field] end) if ok and type(value) == \"number\" then add(string.format(\" %s = %.6f\", field, value)) else add(\" \" .. field .. \" = \u003cunavailable>\") end end local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) add(\" Child actors reported: \" .. tostring(#children)) for i = 1, math.min(#children, config.MaxParts) do local child = object(children[i]) if child then add(\" Part \" .. i .. \": \" .. name(child)) end end if #children > config.MaxParts then add(\" Remaining part names omitted by MaxParts.\") end end local described = 0 local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if valid(pawn) then local ok, gun = pcall(function() return pawn.EquippedWeapon end) if ok and valid(gun) then describe(gun, \"Equipped weapon\"); described = described + 1 end end local editors = FindAllOf(\"WeaponEditMOde_C\") or {} for i = 1, math.min(#editors, config.MaxEditors) do local editor = object(editors[i]) if editor then local ok, visible = pcall(function() return editor:IsVisible() end) if ok and visible then add(\"Visible editor candidate: \" .. name(editor)) describe(editor.CurrentWeapon, \"Workbench weapon\") described = described + 1 end end end if described == 0 then add(\"No equipped gun or visible workshop editor found.\") end add(\"Snapshot finished. No parts, stats or saves were changed by this example.\") return lines end return Inspect Open inspect.lua . Read this module in four stages: Resolve the world. The viewport supplies the active world. A missing world produces a diagnostic result, not an attempt to use an invalid object. Resolve local context. GameplayStatics returns local controller zero. A pawn without EquippedWeapon is normal in some menus. Describe a gun. The module reads three numeric fields independently, then lists child actors up to MaxParts. An unavailable field is reported without writing a replacement value. Inspect editor candidates. Loaded visible WeaponEditMOde widgets can point at a workbench gun. A visible candidate is not proof of unique focus; several can be reported. The wrapper helper accepts an ordinary valid UObject or tries to unwrap a parameter wrapper. The validity helper catches Lua failures while checking a reference. Neither helper protects against every native memory error, so the code still reacquires context for each snapshot instead of caching it across travel. 5. Wire startup and the keybind local Config = require(\"config\") local Log = require(\"log\") local Inspect = require(\"inspect\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be true or false\") assert(type(Config.KeyName) == \"string\" and Key[Config.KeyName], \"KeyName is not a known key\") for _, field in ipairs({\"MaxParts\", \"MaxEditors\"}) do local value = Config[field] assert(type(value) == \"number\" and value % 1 == 0 and value >= 1 and value \u003c= 64, field .. \" must be an integer from 1 to 64\") end if not Config.Enabled then Log.info(\"Disabled in config.lua.\") return end local queued = false RegisterKeyBind(Key[Config.KeyName], function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, result = xpcall(function() return Inspect.capture(Config) end, debug.traceback) queued = false if not ok then Log.info(\"Snapshot failed: \" .. tostring(result)); return end for _, line in ipairs(result) do Log.info(line) end end) end) Log.info(\"Example loaded. Press \" .. Config.KeyName .. \" while the game is focused.\") Open main.lua . Startup validates settings before registering work. The key callback sets a pending flag and queues engine access on the game thread. The flag is released whether the Lua operation succeeds or fails. The logger prints the collected result only after the snapshot function returns. This prevents overlapping queued snapshots. It is not a general-purpose task cancellation system. There is no automatic retry loop because the next key press reacquires the current world and objects. 6. Run and interpret the output Start the game and find [WorkshopInspector] Example loaded. in the log. Open the workbench attachment editor or equip a gun in a range. Focus the game window and press the configured key once. Search for the next [WorkshopInspector] World: message. Compare object names and raw field values, not just the number of printed lines. [WorkshopInspector] World: \u003ccurrent world full name> [WorkshopInspector] Equipped weapon: \u003clive weapon full name> [WorkshopInspector] TotalErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] TotalInstalledErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] Child actors reported: \u003ccount> The workbench route reports a visible editor candidate and a Workbench weapon instead of requiring an Equipped weapon line. A negative raw stat is not automatically a bug; see Weapon stats . 7. Troubleshoot Result Next check No startup line Check enabled.txt, filename extensions, archive root and the fresh loader log. Key does nothing Focus the game; check the configured key and conflicts; confirm main.lua loaded. Viewport/controller not ready Wait until the game has entered a playable menu or range, then press again. No weapon/editor found Open the actual attachment editor or equip a gun; inspect build-specific class names. Stat unavailable Confirm that field exists on the actual weapon class in your build. Snapshot failed Read the traceback; isolate the operation before changing the rest of the module. 8. Extend one thing at a time A useful next step is to collect two snapshots and compare a stat before and after a normal attachment action. Keep only ordinary Lua values, not live actor pointers, in that comparison. Report a changed world or weapon as a different context rather than subtracting unrelated values. For a UI overlay, keep capture separate from rendering and bound its update rate. For a release, follow Packaging a Lua mod . Do not turn this read-only example into an automatic mutation script without adding a clear action, cancellation and recovery flow. Verification limits The game-facing names and integration patterns come from local demo source and earlier native checks. Any mock checks of these files only validate the Lua/control flow they exercise; they do not prove that an actual workbench widget or a future build returns the same data. Use Test a mod step by step to record a real run."},{"id":"testing","title":"Test a mod step by step","text":"Test a mod step by step Prove the smallest claim first, then test the actual player workflow. 1. Write a concrete claim “Works” is too broad to test. Use a trigger and an observable result: “Removing the foregrip restores the installed-ergo baseline,” or “Closing the attachment editor hides the added button.” Write the expected result before changing the code. For a stat issue, a negative final value is not enough; compare it with the same assembly’s starting value. 2. Record the environment Game / build: UE4SS version / commit: Mod version / archive checksum: Other enabled mods: Map and menu: Weapon / rail / attachment class: Reproduction steps: Expected result: Observed result: Relevant log excerpt: Use exact class or display names where possible. Save a fresh log after reproducing, not a file from last week. Remove personal paths and network IDs before posting it publicly. 3. Check pure Lua logic Configuration validation, pagination boundaries, score deduplication and packet parsing can often be tested without Unreal objects. Give them ordinary values and assert the returned result. local seen = {} local function accept(id) if seen[id] then return false end seen[id] = true return true end assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == true) assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == false) assert(accept(\"round1:shot2\") == true) A mock can establish that your callback releases a busy flag after a Lua error. It cannot establish that an engine object is safe to dereference. State the distinction in the result. 4. Use native fixtures for engine behaviour A native fixture uses actual engine objects with controlled ownership. For a child-actor removal test, create a disposable component and point the test widget at it rather than touching the player’s saved gun. Keep a list of every actor, widget, hook and temporary file the fixture owns. Clean those up on success and on Lua failure. Do not stop an unrelated game process or alter another mod’s configuration as an unrecorded side effect. 5. Test the actual workflow Open the normal menu the player uses. Trigger the action through its real input path. Repeat it after the UI rebuilds. Change the weapon or close the menu while work is pending. Travel and come back. For a persisted change, restart and load a disposable save. A widget created by code can pass construction checks while still being off-screen or blocked by another panel. The real input path is a separate check. 6. Add a two-PC matrix Scenario Check on both screens Both fire at once Two distinct shot IDs and correct owner attribution. Same target hit No duplicate reward; consistent final target state. Switch guns Correct model and handling state; no orphaned meshes. Restart / repeated travel Both arrive with usable loadouts and reset registries. Disconnect near round end Clear cancellation or recovery; no old result applied. Save snapshot Consent, backup and successful reload on a disposable guest profile. Use separate Steam accounts and real peers for this stage. Simulated packet delay is useful for repeatability, but it is not a replacement for an actual session. 7. Write the result narrowly Record what ran, what passed, what failed and what the test did not cover. “Ten synthetic removals returned to baseline” is useful. “All attachment bugs fixed” does not follow from it. Once the relevant checks pass, save the tested artifact and its checksum. Re-run checks when code or an unresolved concern justifies it; repeating the same successful simulation does not broaden its scope."},{"id":"weapons","title":"Weapon assemblies","text":"Weapon assemblies A visible gun is an assembly of actors and components, not necessarily one mesh. Find the equipped weapon In a range, the local pawn exposes EquippedWeapon . The workshop uses its own displayed weapon reference, so don’t expect the range pawn to identify the gun on the workbench. local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if not pawn or not pawn:IsValid() then return end local gun = pawn.EquippedWeapon if not gun or not gun:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Equipped: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end) end Parts and components Observed weapon classes include C_WeaponBase , C_WeaponPart and the Blueprint A_WeaponBase_C . Parts expose a ParentWeapon reference. ChildActorComponents can own attached part actors, which can have their own descendants. Enumerate the assembly before changing it. A core barrel, a fitted rail and a nested foregrip can live at different depths. Reading just the root actor’s mesh misses much of the weapon. local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) for i = 1, math.min(#children, 128) do local child = children[i] if child and child:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Part: \" .. child:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Templates are different from live actors CurrentWeaponTemplate records a selected assembly. A spawned weapon is the live actor built from it. Changing the visible actor alone does not establish that the saved template changed, and changing a template does not automatically refresh every existing actor. The observed template has separate weapon-class, main-part, attachment and grip data. The generated field names have suffixes; inspect the current struct rather than copying offsets. Visible doesn’t mean usable A mesh proxy can show another player’s gun without implementing its firing mechanism. A usable gun also needs the correct actor class, parts, ammunition, ownership and input path. Test local use separately from remote appearance. Likewise, hiding a source gun and drawing a replacement mesh does not make a new inventory weapon. Test an assembly Record the selected template and visible part tree. Change one part and confirm only the expected branch changes. Leave the workbench and test handling and firing. Travel to a range and back. Restart and check the saved assembly separately. Walkthrough: inspect one complete assembly 1. Prepare a known gun Use a gun and normal part combination you can identify in the game. Record the selected base weapon, core parts, fitted attachments and currently gripped part. Keep a screenshot of the workbench view so a later actor list has a visual reference. Do not begin with an already complicated cross-weapon assembly. A shorter part tree makes it easier to identify which object was added by one operation. 2. Find the live gun for this context In a range, read the local pawn’s EquippedWeapon. In the attachment editor, inspect the editor’s CurrentWeapon. Log the world and full object name beside each snapshot. A class default object has defaults; it is not the gun the player is holding. If the object is missing, report “not ready” and stop the sample. Creating a replacement weapon just to make inspection return something changes the question you were trying to answer. 3. Capture the tree, not just a count List the root and descendant actors with a bounded output limit. For a deeper investigation, inspect each child’s owner/component relationship and any ParentWeapon reference. A flat list is enough to prove an actor exists, but it does not explain which rail owns a nested grip. Keep copied names and selected numeric values as the baseline. Reacquire live actors for the next sample. Holding old references across a rebuild can turn a useful comparison tool into the source of the failure. 4. Change one ordinary part Use the game’s normal UI to replace or remove one attachment. Capture a second snapshot after the action and compare the changed branch. Record which actors disappeared, which were recreated and whether the main gun identity stayed the same. A recreated actor can have the same class but a different instance suffix. Compare class identity when asking “is this the same type of part?” and instance identity when asking “is this the same live object?”. 5. Separate visible, usable and saved state State How to check it Visible assembly The displayed geometry and captured child tree match. Usable assembly Handling, aiming, magazine interaction and firing work in the range. Selected template The game’s selected loadout reconstructs the intended parts. Persisted assembly A restart and disposable-save reload reconstruct the intended parts. Passing the first row does not establish the other three. This distinction is especially important for model proxies and Lua-only replacement previews. Understanding relative transforms A remote weapon display needs each visible mesh in a common local coordinate system. Copying world positions directly produces offsets that depend on where the source player stood. The local implementation captured mesh positions relative to a weapon-root transform, then rebuilt that relationship for the visual proxy. A complete mesh capture also considers rotation, scale, deduplication and meshes on child actors. Verify the reconstructed assembly while moving, aiming and changing weapons; a correct static screenshot is only the first check. Troubleshooting the result If the root gun appears but attachments do not, inspect child-actor traversal and component selection. If the model appears late, inspect asset-loading and capture cadence. If it is offset or facing backwards, compare transform spaces before adding arbitrary constants. If the local usable gun disappears after travel, inspect loadout reconstruction separately from the friend’s cosmetic proxy. What was checked EquippedWeapon lookup and assembled mesh capture were used in local range fixtures. Arbitrary replacement weapons, imported models and every possible assembly are not verified. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"attachments","title":"Attachments & rails","text":"Attachments & rails Separate the attachment list, the mounting point and the part fitted to it. The attachment flow The workbench creates Attach_POI_C widgets for attachment points. A point references its AttachPoint component, the current weapon and a popup list. Compatibility lookup decides which attachment classes that list can offer. Entry point Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Resolve compatible mount definitions. OnGetUserMenuContent Build the attachment popup. SetupAttachment Apply a class or remove a fitted part. AttachPoint.ChildActor The actor currently fitted at that point. Nested rails A rail can add mounting points of its own. A grip fitted to the rail is a descendant of that rail, not necessarily a direct child of the base weapon. Removing a parent can invalidate the child actor and its UI at the same time. When iterating points during a clear operation, reacquire the current point list after each change. Don’t keep using an old list after the workbench rebuilds it. Fit and remove through the game For a valid live attachment-point widget, the native action takes a class reference to fit a part. Passing nil removed it in local tests: -- Context: poi is a valid live Attach_POI widget. -- This changes the assembly; it is not an inspection call. poi:SetupAttachment(nil) Let the game handle default-part behaviour and its refresh chain. Directly destroying a child actor bypasses that UI action and may leave the template or derived stats out of date. Compatibility isn’t ownership A part can be mount-compatible but not bought. A shop entry can be visible but locked. A saved assembly may contain a part that a purchased-set-only check misses. Keep these questions separate; see Ownership & unlocks . Test repeated edits Record stats before fitting, after fitting and after removal. Repeat with the same rail, then try removing the rail while a child part is still mounted. Check that a cancelled operation doesn’t remove a part from a newly selected gun. For a stat report, keep the exact weapon, rail and child class names. A vague “negative stat” report is not enough to decide whether an extra subtraction occurred. Walkthrough: trace a fit/remove action 1. Identify the point Open the attachment editor and pick a point whose current part you can name. Record the POI widget, AttachPoint component and ChildActor before doing anything. If it is a rail’s own point, also record the rail actor that owns that branch. Several points can offer the same part classes. The class of an attachment does not uniquely identify the point the player selected. 2. Record what the list means Observe the menu before filtering or pagination. Count real attachment entries separately from the empty-slot icon and any tab row your mod adds. Decide whether your feature changes compatibility, ownership visibility or just presentation. Those changes should not be mixed into one undocumented “filter”. For tabbed lists, partition the original item list, then add your navigation controls outside that item count. Otherwise reopening or rebuilding the list can accidentally count your own tab bar as another attachment. 3. Fit one known-compatible part Use the normal click action first and confirm the visible child, the refreshed point list and a stat snapshot. If that action fails without your mod, investigate the base setup before expanding compatibility. A mutating Lua action should use the intended game path and verified class type. Passing an object instance where a class reference is expected is not the same operation. 4. Remove and reacquire After removal, do not use a cached child actor to ask what is now mounted. Reacquire ChildActor from the current AttachPoint. If the editor rebuilt its POIs, reacquire the point list too. For a clear-all action, finish one removal, let the normal refresh chain run and inspect the new list before the next step. Protect the job against duplicate clicks, changed weapon, closed editor and world travel. 5. Test parent removal separately Removing an empty rail and removing a rail with several children are different cases. Observe what happens to each descendant, its stats and its menu points. If the UI rebuild invalidates a child point, the job must skip that old reference rather than force another removal on it. Pagination boundaries Use ordinary Lua item arrays for your page calculation. For a positive page size, an item’s page is floor((index − 1) / pageSize) + 1 . The number of non-empty pages is ceil(itemCount / pageSize) . Decide whether an empty list shows no tabs or a single empty page, then test that policy explicitly. Items at page size 20 Non-empty pages 0 0; an empty-state design is still needed. 1–20 1 21–40 2 41–60 3 Do not add an extra empty tab just because the last page is exactly full unless that is an intentional UI requirement. Keep tab visibility independent from whether the game considers a part owned. Completion check Verify fitting, removing, replacing, parent-rail removal, empty clearing and cancelled clearing. For expanded combinations, test handling and persistence independently. Report a stable list and working tabs as those specific results; they do not prove every offered combination is physically or mechanically valid. What was checked Single native removals, synthetic three-point clear batches and rebuilding/cancellation guards passed local fixtures. These do not prove every real fitted assembly saves and reloads correctly. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"weapon-stats","title":"Weapon stats","text":"Weapon stats Measure a baseline before trying to fix a stat that looks wrong. Part values and weapon totals Field Where it was observed ErgoModifier Per-part ergonomics modifier on C_WeaponPart. TotalErgo Weapon’s current ergonomics total. TotalInstalledErgo Separate installed-part ergonomics total. TotalRecoilControlModifier Weapon recoil-control aggregate. ParentWeapon Part’s associated weapon reference. Current and installed ergonomics are different fields. Don’t substitute one for the other in an overlay. A part may also affect recoil, weight, cooling or grip behaviour rather than just ergonomics. Read a baseline -- Context: gun is a valid live weapon, on the game thread. print(string.format( \"[MyMod] current=%.6f installed=%.6f recoil=%.6f\\n\", gun.TotalErgo, gun.TotalInstalledErgo, gun.TotalRecoilControlModifier )) Recalculation The Blueprint weapon base has UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . Its nine boolean inputs in the inspected build are: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength This is a mutating recalculation function. Confirm the signature in your own dump before calling it. Prefer observing the normal attachment action first, rather than forcing a recalculation on every frame and masking a broken refresh chain. A removal test A local Sten fixture used a barrel strap and nested ergonomic broom grip. The grip’s modifier was approximately +0.15 . Ten fit/remove cycles with the expanded compatibility hook active gave: Step Installed ergo Rail-only baseline −0.600000 Grip fitted −0.450000 Grip removed −0.600000 That fixture started negative and returned to the same negative baseline. It did not show a cumulative penalty. It was a synthetic assembly, so those numbers are not the correct expected totals for every player’s Sten. Report a stat bug Include weapon/rail/attachment names, all three snapshots, game and loader versions, and whether the result survives leaving the workbench or restarting. Compare ordinary and expanded compatibility where possible. Don’t clamp every negative number to zero. That can hide legitimate penalties and conceal the actual bug. Walkthrough: diagnose a reported stat penalty 1. Fix the context Record the game build, loader version, gun class, rail class, child attachment class and grip/stance state. Current ergonomics can differ from an installed total; comparing different fields or different grips can create a false apparent regression. Use a disposable known assembly. Keep the normal baseline before expanding compatibility so the comparison is meaningful. 2. Take three snapshots Capture the same fields in the same context at three points: rail present with no tested child; tested child fitted; tested child removed. Record the actual numbers to enough precision to see small changes. local function compare(before, fitted, removed, epsilon) local bonus = fitted - before local residue = removed - before return { fittedDelta = bonus, removalResidue = residue, returnedToBaseline = math.abs(residue) \u003c= epsilon } end local result = compare(-0.6, -0.45, -0.6, 0.00001) assert(result.returnedToBaseline) The fitted delta describes the child’s observed contribution in that setup. The removal residue is the quantity to investigate for an accumulated penalty. Its sign alone is not sufficient; compare its size with the baseline and an appropriate tolerance. 3. Repeat without masking the refresh path Run several fit/remove cycles through the normal action. Do not manually recalculate after every removal if the question is whether the normal refresh is broken. That could make the symptom disappear without fixing the action chain. Capture a separate “manual recalculation” experiment only if needed, and label it clearly. If manual recalculation restores the baseline but ordinary removal does not, the refresh path becomes a focused investigation target. 4. Compare three lifetimes Check the value immediately after the action, after leaving/re-entering the editor, and after a restart using a disposable saved assembly. A stale displayed label, a stale live aggregate and a persisted template error can produce similar-looking screenshots while requiring different fixes. The recalculation flags in detail Flag Area selected for investigation Weight / CenterMassLoc Mass-related aggregates and location. Ergo / GripCenter Ergonomics and grip-related location. Recoil Recoil modifier aggregates. BarrelLength / TotalLength Weapon dimensions. EquippedScopeAndLaser Equipped sight/laser references. Cooling Cooling-related aggregate. This table groups the observed input names; it is not a recovered implementation of every formula. Check the signature and the code path that normally calls the function before choosing flags. A function that recomputes several systems is not a harmless “refresh text” operation. What a conclusion can say If all repeated removals match the starting value within tolerance, say that the tested setup returned to baseline. If another player still has a different assembly, keep that report open until its exact setup is reproduced. If a residue appears, identify which field and lifecycle first diverged before writing a clamp or an extra subtraction. What was checked The ten-cycle fixture ran on 6 November 2026 and returned to baseline without manual refresh between cycles. The reported real-player rail-removal penalty remains unconfirmed. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"ballistics","title":"Shots & ballistics","text":"Shots & ballistics A fired shot, a projectile impact and a valid target hit are separate events. The shot pipeline Event What it tells you A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Weapon firing event with cartridge, barrel and created bullet information. C_BulletBase:RegisterHit A bullet reported impact information. TargetBase_C:Hit Target hit path with projectile attribution in the inspected signature. TargetBase_C:OnHit Target reports validity, energy, velocity and bonus-related flags. A projectile can strike non-target geometry. A target callback can be invalid. A shot counter and a scoring system should not increment from the same undifferentiated “hit” event. Observe firing Once the Blueprint function is loaded, a read-only hook can establish when it runs: local path = \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired\" -- Register once, after StaticFindObject(path) is valid. RegisterHook(path, function(context) local gun = context:get() if gun and gun:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Fired: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end) Observed shot arguments After the context, the inspected firing signature has Cartridge , bullet and gas recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel , powder amount, exit pressure, powder type and a Bullet array. Read the exact signature and unwrap parameters according to their type before using them. Do not assume one event always produces exactly one bullet actor. Keep a shot ID and map each resulting projectile to it. Impacts, energy and units The target’s OnHit signature contains HitEnergy and HitVelocity , but a field name alone does not establish the units or all conversions used by the game. Record the raw values and compare controlled shots before labelling them in an overlay. Don’t replace the game’s trajectory with a camera ray and call it the actual bullet path. A straight line can be useful as a cosmetic tracer, but it does not reproduce drop, penetration or ricochets. What is not documented here This guide maps events, not the complete internal ballistics model. Cartridge construction, powder curves, drag coefficients, penetration materials and every ammunition unlock rule still need dedicated research. For score handling, continue to Hits & scoring . Walkthrough: connect a shot to an impact 1. Start with one slow, controlled shot Choose a gun and cartridge, keep the target distance fixed and fire once. Log a firing-event count and the weapon identity. Confirm a single trigger produces the expected firing callbacks before adding an impact counter. Do not use a rapid-fire sequence to discover attribution. Several projectiles in flight make “the latest shot” an unreliable shortcut immediately. 2. Read the firing signature Observed parameter Meaning to investigate Cartridge The cartridge object associated with the firing event. BulletRecoilForce / GasRecoilForce Two recoil-force inputs; not a substitute for muzzle velocity. RecoilLocation / RecoilDirection Location and direction inputs for recoil processing. Barrel The firing barrel object. PowderAmount / ExitPressure / PowderType Ammunition-related inputs with build-specific interpretation. Bullet Array of resulting projectile references. Keep raw values and reflected types in a research note. Do not infer units, valid ranges or a complete ballistic formula from these labels. 3. Assign a shot identity Increment a local shot number at the firing event. Associate every resulting bullet reference with that number and a timestamp. One shot can produce several projectile actors; a bullet’s runtime address is useful only inside the current process/world lifetime. For a network message, send an explicit shot ID and session/round context. Never send a local address and expect the other process to have an object at that address. 4. Observe impacts separately RegisterHit reports bullet impact information. Use the bullet-to-shot mapping to identify which shot produced it. A wall impact can matter for a tracer or replay without being a scoring target hit. Penetration or multiple contacts also make “one impact equals one shot” a weak assumption. 5. Observe the target decision Read the target’s valid-hit decision and attribution before awarding a custom score. A target can report a contact that is invalid for scoring, has already been spent, or belongs to a different player/round. Preserve the game’s own progression path. Cosmetic replication should not blindly replay a reward-granting callback. A measurement log that stays interpretable session / world: weapon and barrel class: cartridge identity: shot ID and creation time: projectile identities: impact order and target identities: raw hit energy / velocity: valid-hit and breaking-hit flags: whether any values were converted for display: Use a fixed distance and change one ammunition or barrel variable at a time. If two values differ, first check that they came from the same event and coordinate/time basis. Cosmetic effects and real trajectories A short-lived muzzle flash, sound or straight tracer can communicate that a friend fired. It does not establish the actual projectile’s full path. Keep effect lifetime bounded and clean up the actors/components your effect owns. To document drop, penetration or ricochet accurately, gather projectile/impact samples and the game’s relevant configuration. This wiki currently maps useful entry points and the investigation method; it does not claim those internal systems have all been reconstructed. What was checked Firing and impact hooks resolved in local range checks; native target hit callbacks were also exercised. No full trajectory or ammunition-physics replacement has been validated. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"targets","title":"Finding & tracking targets","text":"Finding & tracking targets Find the range’s target actors, then inspect the component that actually moves. Discover targets The inspected range uses TargetBase_C and derived classes. Get the active world from the viewport, then query that world: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset( \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\" ) if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) print(\"[MyMod] Targets found: \" .. #targets .. \"\\n\") end Useful fields Field Observed use HasBeenHit Whether the target has been marked hit. ValidForOneHitOnly Reject further scoring hits after the first. HitValue Current target value. MinHitValue Lower bound used when reducing a value. HitValueMultOnHit Multiplier used by the local hit-state implementation. isMoving Hint used to prioritise moving target samples. Actor versus component motion A physics mesh can move while its owning actor’s transform stays unchanged. Capturing only K2_GetActorLocation can therefore show a target standing upright remotely while its mesh has fallen over locally. Inspect the primitive, static-mesh or skeletal-mesh component that carries the visible body. Capture its world transform separately. Different target classes can need different component selection. Sampling and caching Discover actors infrequently and sample known targets on a bounded budget. Give moving and recently hit targets priority. A local registry once contained hundreds of targets; rediscovering or loading assets for every target on every frame is unnecessary. Invalidate the registry after travel. Runtime addresses identify local objects only; they are not stable network IDs. Resetting targets Clearing HasBeenHit alone does not restore a broken target, a displaced physics body, its collision, or a reduced score value. A complete reset needs the original state of each relevant field and component. Restarting the range can be a more reliable first reset implementation. Walkthrough: build a target registry 1. Enter a known range Use the normal range flow and confirm the actual loaded level. A menu world can have no targets, and a table row named after a range does not prove that range was loaded. Resolve the world through the current viewport before querying actors. 2. Discover once and inspect a few examples Get actors of the known base class, then log a bounded sample of full names and actual classes. A derived class can have a different body component, physics setup or hit implementation. Keep a registry with an explicit cap and explain what happens if the cap is exceeded. 3. Select the visible body Enumerate primitive components and identify the mesh that represents the moving target. Record its component name, class and initial world transform. Avoid selecting an unrelated trigger box only because it is the first primitive component returned. For a falling pan or moving figure, compare actor location and mesh location while the motion occurs. If only the mesh moves, actor-only replication will visibly fail even though the actor transform is being sent correctly. 4. Save a plain-data baseline Store copied transforms and the specific flags your mode needs: hit state, value, hidden/collision state and physics state where relevant. Keep the live object reference separately and invalidate it with the world. A baseline is a record of what you observed. It is not a guarantee that resetting those fields is sufficient for every target class; some targets can have additional state or event dependencies. 5. Update with a bounded budget Carry a cursor through ordinary entries and separately sample moving/recently hit entries with a cap. Record the worst time between samples for an ordinary target. A registry of hundreds of targets makes that delay measurable; “we update some targets each tick” is not enough to judge motion quality. If you interpolate a remote component, keep its render pose separate from the received authoritative sample. Treat a teleport or reset as a discontinuity rather than interpolating through the entire range. 6. Verify reset behaviour Hit a normal target, hit a one-shot target, knock over a physics target and trigger a breakable target separately. Compare their required reset state. If a complete reset is not understood, implement a range restart instead of offering a reset button that restores only a flag. Value and presentation can diverge A target’s current HitValue can decrease after an accepted hit. A custom challenge may deliberately use a value captured at round start for fairness. Document which policy you chose. Reading a new value after applying the game’s hit reduction can otherwise score the wrong amount. For one-hit targets, check both ValidForOneHitOnly and HasBeenHit. For hidden or destroyed targets, reject further custom hit processing rather than awarding points from a stale registry entry. Lifetime tests Discover and count in the known world. Capture a body transform before and during motion. Trigger a valid hit and verify the expected state change. Restart the range and build a fresh registry. Confirm no old-world actor is sampled or mutated. What was checked Local discovery, target baselines, distinct synthetic targets and actor/component motion were tested. The documented registry strategy is a mod implementation, not a built-in replication service. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"scoring","title":"Hits & scoring","text":"Hits & scoring Count one valid gameplay event once, and keep your own score separate from progression. Target callbacks The observed OnHit signature passes ValidHit , player, impact information, hit energy, hit velocity, bonus flag, cartridge-unlock flag and breaking-hit flag. Character targets have their own matching hook path in the inspected build. Inspect validity before scoring. Registering both base and derived handlers without attribution or deduplication can count a single physical shot more than once. Attribute a hit Give each local firing event a monotonically increasing shot ID. Associate created bullet actors with that shot. Use the target’s hit path to connect the bullet and target. Check valid-hit state before adding a score. Retire old shot and bullet references after a short bounded window. Do not attribute every hit to “the most recent shot” when the weapon can have several projectiles in flight. Two players firing at once makes that mistake especially visible. Deduplicate events For a custom per-target hit score, one useful key is round + player + shot + target . Decide explicitly whether pellets or repeated contacts should score independently for your mode. Deduplication policy is game design, not just a networking detail. -- Custom mode state, not the game's persistent score. local seen = {} local score = 0 local function award(roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId, points) local key = table.concat({roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId}, \":\") if seen[key] then return false end if type(points) ~= \"number\" or points ~= points then return false end if points \u003c 0 or points > 100000 then return false end seen[key] = true score = score + math.floor(points) return true end Cosmetics are not rewards A replicated impact can play a reaction without replaying the full local scoring/reward event. The local target fixture used CosmeticOnHit for visual response, with custom score handled separately. That does not make the function a complete substitute for the game’s normal hit logic. Do not replay the ordinary hit event merely to display a remote impact: it may grant progression, unlock ammunition or change target value again. Round timing Use explicit countdown, playing, settling and finished phases. For network play, define whether a hit near the deadline is accepted by host receipt time, synchronised event time or a bounded grace policy. Cancel or pause when the connection changes instead of letting an invisible round keep running. Walkthrough: design a small scoring mode 1. Write the rules before the hook Decide whether a score is per projectile, per shot, per target or per round. Decide how repeated hits and pellets behave, whether a spent target scores again, and whether a target’s value is fixed at round start or read live. These rules determine your identifiers and deduplication key. 2. Log eligible events without awarding Observe firing and target callbacks. Record player identity, shot ID, target identity, valid-hit state and event time. Confirm the same physical action is not entering both a base and derived handler as two awards. Start with one player and one target, then two distinct targets, then rapid shots. Networking should come after local attribution is understandable. 3. Keep custom score in pure state Use a Lua state object for round ID, phase, totals and the seen-event set. Do not store a custom challenge’s points in an unrelated game progression field just because it is already visible somewhere. Validate finite bounded numbers before adding them. Reject malformed IDs and stale round identifiers. Define a maximum session total if packets or logs use a limited numeric representation. 4. Implement explicit phases Phase Allowed behaviour Countdown Show the start time; do not award the competitive round yet. Playing Accept eligible shots and valid hit events. Settling Accept only events permitted by the documented end-of-round timing policy. Finished Show result and permit a rematch action. Cancelled Explain the interruption and require an explicit restart. A settling phase is not permission to accept arbitrary late points. Preserve the event’s original eligible time, round identity and a bounded acceptance window. The local implementation has such a policy; internet fairness still needs real delayed-peer testing. 5. Choose score authority For a host-authoritative challenge, the guest submits an attributed event and the host decides whether to apply it. Both peers display the host’s accepted round state. The guest should not independently overwrite the shared total when another snapshot arrives. Connection to Steam does not supply this rule automatically. It is part of the gameplay protocol you implement. 6. Handle interruptions and rematches Cancel or pause when a participant disconnects, becomes unready or leaves the world. Include the reason in the displayed state. On rematch, increment round identity and clear the relevant dedupe state so an old packet cannot award points to the new round. Acceptance cases Test an ordinary hit, an invalid hit, a duplicate event, an already-spent target, two players hitting the same target, a stale round, a hit near the deadline and a reconnect. Confirm cosmetic effects do not grant points twice and a rematch begins with new identifiers. Keep screenshots of the displayed result and a small event trace. A correct final total alone can hide two wrong awards that happened to cancel each other. What was checked Local fixtures exercised valid native hits, one-hit rejection, cosmetic reactions and separate session scores. Internet-delay fairness and simultaneous real-peer shooting are separate tests, not proven by a local fixture. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"ranges","title":"Range menus & travel","text":"Range menus & travel A range card is UI data. A playable destination also needs a cooked map and a working travel path. Range menu data The range table is /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData . The PistolRange row mapped to Range_SwimmingPool in local checks. Its display label is Azure Swimming Pool. The inspected table also had rows such as Valley and ShoppingMall . Their presence did not prove those maps were usable in the demo. What makes a card appear? Two observed UI entry points are RangeList.PreConstruct and RangeListItem.SetSelected . The menu also checks the game instance’s UnlockedRanges , so adding a row alone may leave the card absent. A runtime-only custom card was made visible locally by adding its row name to the in-memory unlocked list. That was an experimental menu change, not a demonstrated permanent unlock or newly cooked map. Confirm the destination Level Package / result Bunker_Main /Game/Maps/Bunker/Bunker_Main Range_SwimmingPool /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool Range_Valley A custom-card travel attempt returned to the main menu; not a working demo destination. Use a small explicit allowlist of destinations you tested. Don’t assemble arbitrary map paths from a user-entered label or assume every table row exists in the archive. Local and shared travel Opening a map for one local player is different from moving a connected listen-server session. The local multiplayer code used the AdvancedSessions ServerTravel path with bUseSeamlessTravel enabled and a ?listen destination. That solution depends on the session and driver already being valid. Appending ?listen to an arbitrary OpenLevel call is not a complete multiplayer implementation. After arrival Confirm the actual current level name and world identity. Reacquire controllers, pawns, weapons, targets and UI. Confirm the local player has a usable weapon and ammunition. For multiplayer, confirm both peers arrived before starting a round. Try restart, return to bunker and repeat travel. Building a detached scene by teleporting within the pool world still uses that world’s lighting and lifecycle. See Custom content . Walkthrough: investigate a custom range entry 1. Verify the normal selection flow Start without the custom entry and follow the actual Enter Range and confirmation flow. Record which screen constructs the list and what selecting a working range does. A confirmation screen is not itself the range list; hooking the wrong stage can miss the actual widgets. 2. Inspect one working row Read the known PistolRange row and its map field. Record the display label, row name, destination ID and verified package separately. Do not use the display label as a package path. 3. Separate visibility from selection If an added row is absent, inspect the list’s unlock check and row iteration. If it appears but cannot be selected, inspect the card’s selection state and callback. If it is selected but travel fails, inspect the destination and travel call. During the recorded runtime-card work, bounded numeric iteration avoided an out-of-bounds iterator freeze. That was a container-binding failure, not a reason to keep enlarging or recolouring the card. 4. Confirm the destination by entering it Check the actual current level after travel. A card can have the desired title and still route to the swimming pool. That is a successful card prototype, not a new playable map. An unavailable destination returning to the main menu should be recorded as a failed travel result. Don’t keep advertising the destination solely because its table row exists. 5. If building a runtime scene, prove placement first Spawn one known mesh in a clearly visible position relative to the player and log the requested and actual transform. Confirm mobility permits the intended operation. Expand the layout gradually, with a safe spawn and an escape/return route. Placing buildings far beyond an indoor pool’s walls can produce a successful spawn log and nothing visible. The requested coordinates, player facing, source-target distance and occlusion all matter. 6. Test presentation independently Verify sky, ambient light, materials, exposure and shadowing in the host world. If the player’s gun is bright but distant runtime structures are dark silhouettes, that is useful evidence against a simple “everything was culled” explanation. Inspect the render context before trying another arbitrary view-distance value. A shared range-arrival handshake For multiplayer, distinguish request, countdown, travel, arrival, object rebuild and ready-to-play phases. The host should not start a round merely because its own loading screen finished. Confirm the guest’s world generation and relevant target/loadout state too. On failure, expose a recovery action such as retry, restart or return to bunker. A hidden retry loop that repeatedly travels both peers can turn one bad destination into a confusing session failure. Verification sequence Test normal entry, custom-card selection, arrival identity, spawn safety, visible targets, usable guns, exit, restart and repeated travel. For a true custom cooked map, document its package/loading pipeline separately from runtime geometry inside an existing map. What was checked Pool travel, restart and return were exercised in local native/listen fixtures. Two-PC travel reliability and absent demo maps are not implied by those checks. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"workshop-ui","title":"Workshop UI","text":"Workshop UI Reuse the game’s widgets, but create your own controls and callbacks. Find the workshop The observed HUD class is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is WeaponEditMOde , with that exact spelling. The HUD and attachment editor are related but have different lifetimes. A button placed on the HUD canvas needs its visibility tied to entering and leaving attachment edit mode. Create a native button The common TheRangeButton_C class provides the game’s existing button appearance. This creates a separate widget; it does not click or duplicate Save and exit: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/UI/Common/TheRangeButton.TheRangeButton_C\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local library = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/UMG.Default__WidgetBlueprintLibrary\") local button = library:Create(pc, class, pc) if not button or not button:IsValid() then return end button.Text = FText(\"My action\") button.NavText:SetText(FText(\"My action\")) -- Add button to your valid parent panel and retain a reference. end Place the control For a CanvasPanel parent, add with AddChildToCanvas and set the resulting CanvasPanelSlot’s anchors, alignment, position and size. Position numbers are relative to the anchors, not always screen pixels from the top left. A local workbench check found Save and exit at X=50, Y=-50 , size 200×40 , with bottom-left anchors and bottom alignment. A button directly above it used Y=-100 , leaving a ten-unit gap. Those numbers describe that HUD layout, not every resolution or future build. Don’t copy action delegates Copy appearance properties deliberately, not the original widget’s action bindings. A copied Save and exit delegate can execute the original action when your new button is pressed. One local implementation handled the left-mouse keybind and checked the native button’s hover state. That verified a mouse path only; it does not establish controller, keyboard, touch or accessibility support. Prefer a verified button event route when your loader and widget expose one. Rebuilds and input Prevent duplicate construction by checking that your widget still has a valid parent. Hide it outside edit mode, cancel pending actions when the displayed weapon changes, and restore any input mode or cursor state you changed. Test hover, click, closing the menu and opening it again. A widget object existing in memory does not prove that it is visible or clickable. Walkthrough: add a control without stealing another action 1. Observe the normal editor lifecycle Open and close attachment edit mode normally. Log HUD construction, edit-mode entry/exit and editor refreshes. Determine which parent panel survives an attachment change. If the point canvas is cleared during refresh, a button placed inside it may be removed with the points. 2. Create a fresh widget Use the native button class for appearance, but instantiate it separately. Set its text and supported appearance fields deliberately. Do not clone the original HUD button’s delegates or use a raw object copy that carries a Save and exit callback into your new action. Keep a strong, reachable reference through the owning widget tree/panel while the control is displayed. A local Lua variable that falls out of scope is not your complete UI ownership strategy. 3. Understand the canvas coordinates With bottom-left anchors, negative Y positions move upward relative to the bottom edge. Alignment determines which point of the widget is placed at that coordinate. Copy anchors, alignment and size together before calculating a vertical offset. Test the actual black menu bounds at more than one window size. Changing only X/Y can look correct at one resolution while breaking when the canvas scales or the text changes. 4. Make installation idempotent Before creating another button, check whether the existing widget is valid and still parented. Reuse it when possible. If the original panel was rebuilt, attach a replacement once and update the stored reference. Idempotent means repeating the setup produces one control in the correct state, not another copy of it. 5. Bind one action and a busy state Disable the control while a queued operation is active. Recheck the editor/weapon context inside the operation. On completion, cancellation or Lua failure, update the text and enabled state consistently. If your only verified input method is a hovered-button mouse check, document that limitation. A visible native button does not automatically give your custom Lua action keyboard/controller support. 6. Tie visibility to edit mode Hide the control on edit-mode exit and cancel pending work that depends on that mode. Re-entry should restore the appropriate enabled state, not always assume a previous job succeeded. Check behaviour when the player switches weapons without leaving the HUD. UI acceptance matrix Action Expected result Open editor One control appears inside the intended panel. Rebuild attachment points The control remains or is recreated once. Click while busy No duplicate job starts. Switch weapon Old job stops before affecting the new assembly. Close editor Control hides and context-dependent work stops. Resize / reopen Placement, label and focus remain usable. Keep action verification separate from appearance checks. A button with matching font and hover styling can still call the wrong action. What was checked Native button construction, matching canvas layout, duplicate prevention and edit-mode visibility passed local fixtures. Full interaction on every fitted player weapon and input device was not established. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"progression","title":"Ownership & unlocks","text":"Ownership & unlocks “Visible”, “unlocked”, “purchased” and “fitted” are different states. The relevant containers Game-instance field Observed representation PurchasedParts Set of soft class references for purchased parts. UnlockedWeapons Array of soft weapon-class references. UnlockedRanges Array of range row names. SavedWeaponTemplates Map containing saved weapon assemblies. A template can contain part paths that aren’t matched by a purchased-set-only check. That does not prove those paths all represent independently owned, currently available attachments. Why simple filters fail Three local attachment-menu experiments illustrate the problem: A button-enabled check let every entry through, so enabled state did not express ownership. PurchasedParts alone matched far fewer menu entries than the player expected. A union of purchased and saved-template paths still missed expected choices in the live menu. Those filters were not a completed ownership API. They were removed from the stable expanded catalog. Match reference types Passing a loaded UClass directly to a soft-reference set caused a native crash in an earlier filter. An isolated class-to-soft-reference conversion probe was safe for one unrelated class, but that did not validate every menu membership check. Inspect the stored type, the API’s expected type and the exact path form. Package paths, object paths and generated Blueprint class paths are related but not identical strings. Design an owned-only menu Define whether “owned” includes default, bought and currently fitted parts. Start with a small set of attachments whose ownership the player can confirm. Compare those parts with the game’s normal menu. Use stable identifiers and explain each exclusion in a diagnostic log. Test pagination after filtering, including an empty result and a single page. Do not present “we found many save paths” as proof the filter is correct. The list the player can actually select is the test that matters. Unlocking content Adding an unlocked row name in memory can make a menu card appear. It doesn’t establish permanent progression, the presence of the associated map, or correct save persistence. A production unlock mod needs those checks separately. Walkthrough: investigate one missing owned part 1. Define “owned” for the feature Write down whether the list includes default parts, explicitly purchased parts, parts fitted to saved templates and content merely available in the shop. Those definitions can produce different correct lists. Get the expected rule clear before implementing membership. 2. Build a tiny ground-truth set Pick a few named parts the player can demonstrate as bought or fitted, plus a few definitely unowned parts. Capture their normal menu entries and the relevant saved/live representations. A 137-entry filtered catalog is harder to debug than six known examples. 3. Record stored types and path forms PurchasedParts uses soft class references in the inspected game instance; an attachment widget can expose a loaded class. Compare these representations deliberately. Record the package/object/generated-class path instead of normalising all strings by blindly deleting _C. A native container membership call can be binding-sensitive. The prior wrong-type call crashed the process; a Lua pcall is not a native pointer-safety mechanism. 4. Explain every inclusion decision part=\u003cstable path> normal_menu_state=\u003cobserved state> matched_purchased=\u003cyes/no/not checked> matched_saved_template=\u003cyes/no/not checked> is_default=\u003cknown/unknown> final_decision=\u003cinclude/exclude/unknown> reason=\u003cspecific rule> Represent unknown explicitly while investigating. Quietly treating an unreadable reference as unowned hides exactly the case you need to inspect. 5. Validate the live list Open multiple real attachment points, compare the expected named parts, switch tabs and fit one allowed part. Then check an unowned control. A plausible save-index count does not establish correct live filtering. Counts are not correctness In the local experiments, purchased-only membership and a larger union with template paths both produced unexpectedly small lists. The player’s expected part names remained the deciding test. Avoid promising six pages just because an index contains enough paths; several paths can refer to content not represented by the current menu. Persistence check If you change an unlock container in memory, leave and re-enter the menu, then restart using a disposable save. Determine whether the change was transient or persisted by a game action. Do not advertise permanent unlocking from a successful in-memory card visibility check. What was checked The container types and failed filtering outcomes are recorded local observations. A complete reliable owned-only attachment filter remains unresolved. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"saves","title":"Reading & backing up saves","text":"Reading & backing up saves Start read-only. A live game instance and a save file are not the same snapshot. Save location The local demo uses %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/BallisticDemo.sav . Close the game and copy the save folder somewhere separate before an experiment that can change progression or assemblies. Keep backups out of your mod release and out of a public repository. A useful backup is a dated copy you can identify and restore, not a file overwritten on every test. Load without applying GameplayStatics can load the persisted slot into a save object. Loading it for inspection is different from replacing the game instance’s active state: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local instance = gameplay:GetGameInstance(world) if not instance or not instance:IsValid() then return end local save = gameplay:LoadGameFromSlot(\"BallisticDemo\", instance.UserIndex) if save and save:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Save class: \" .. save:GetClass():GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Known save class /Game/BlueprintLogic/Core/Game/Save/BallisticSaveGame.BallisticSaveGame_C Do not assume that copying properties from one object to another is a complete restore. The local save-service code also refreshes game-instance data and invalidates selected loadouts and cached range lists after applying a snapshot. Snapshot versus live state A file copy contains the last persisted state. Unsaved workbench edits or in-memory progression can differ. “Host save to guest” is a one-way snapshot transfer unless you explicitly implement a merge or ongoing replication. A sharing feature should distinguish accepting a download from applying it. Bind the final confirmation to the exact peer and transfer, reject incompatible data, back up the guest’s original file, and handle failed loads with a recovery path. Verify a save feature Use disposable profiles for write tests. Record a file hash before a read-only probe and compare it afterwards. For a restore feature, test a controlled backup/apply/restore cycle and then restart the game. A success message from a copy operation does not prove the game can load the result. Walkthrough: inspect without applying 1. Make an identifiable backup Close the game, copy the save folder to a separate dated location and keep the original filenames. Record the source file’s size, modification time and checksum if your test needs to prove no file changed. A backup stored beside a release staging folder can accidentally be shipped. Keep private save material outside the build allowlist and review the archive contents before sharing. 2. Decide which state you want to inspect LoadGameFromSlot reads the persisted slot. The game instance contains current in-memory state. If the player edited a gun but has not saved, the two can legitimately differ. Describe the chosen snapshot in the tool’s UI and logs. 3. Read the class before the fields Use the read-only loading example, confirm the resulting class and inspect a small known field. Do not immediately assign the loaded object to CurrentSaveGame or call a retrieval/apply function; that crosses from inspection into changing the active game state. A field whose name appears in a binary save is a discovery hint. It is not by itself a complete schema or proof that a byte-level edit will remain loadable. 4. Compare before and after For a read-only probe, compare the original file checksum after the test and note any ordinary in-game actions that could have saved independently. For a write test, use a disposable profile and record exactly which operation changed the file. Designing a snapshot transfer Stage Required distinction Offer Host identifies a bounded last-saved snapshot. Accept download Guest consents to receive bytes, not to overwrite progression. Receive / verify Length, format, transfer identity and integrity are checked. Apply confirmation Guest explicitly confirms applying this exact transfer from this peer. Backup / replace Original local data is preserved before replacement. Reload / refresh Game state and cached selections are updated or recovery is triggered. Do not call a one-time file snapshot “shared live progression”. It does not merge simultaneous changes or keep both saves continuously identical. Applying and recovering Apply only in a stable context, with travel and another transfer blocked. Bind the confirmation to the transfer ID so a delayed click cannot apply a newly arrived offer accidentally. If the imported state fails to load, restore the preserved backup and report the failure. After a successful reload, invalidate cached selected weapons, unlocked range lists and UI data that were derived from the previous save. A correct file on disk can still leave the current menu showing stale selections. What to test before releasing a save feature Test valid input, malformed input, oversized input, interruption, reconnect, cancelled acceptance, repeated apply confirmation and failed load/recovery. Then restart and confirm the disposable profile loads. Local transfer simulation can validate a state machine without proving a real guest’s backup/apply flow. What was checked Read-only slot loading and native export checks ran locally. Local save-sharing code exists, but real two-PC guest application was not verified in the recorded test pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"multiplayer","title":"Multiplayer architecture","text":"Multiplayer architecture Connecting two players is the beginning. Shared game state still needs explicit rules. Connection isn’t replication A Steam session or invitation identifies and connects peers. It does not automatically make every single-player actor, UI action, weapon part or score replicate. The local multiplayer implementation combines a listen-server travel path with a native Steam bridge and custom state messages. That architecture is a mod implementation, not an official multiplayer API supplied by this wiki. Separate state from events Send as state Send as events Player pose, aim and equipped appearance A shot, a hit or a chat message. Target pose and current hit state A round-start or recovery request. Current round phase and scores A one-time save offer or acceptance. Snapshots can supersede older snapshots. One-time events need IDs, acknowledgement and duplicate handling. Replaying the same acknowledged hit must not award its points again. Authority and validation Choose who decides travel, target state and scoring. A host-authoritative mode should validate the sender, session, round, target identity and bounded event values before applying a guest request. Never accept arbitrary asset paths, file paths or Lua source from a network message. Use explicit allowlists and fixed save slots where appropriate. Movement and visible guns Render remote movement from buffered snapshots rather than snapping directly to each packet. Reset the buffer for teleport or world changes. Interpolation hides normal packet gaps; it does not fix wrong authority or missing state. Remote weapon appearance can be reconstructed from mesh references and relative transforms. Reuse unchanged meshes and clean up replaced ones. A cosmetic proxy is not another fully simulated gun actor. Interruptions and testing Give every session and world generation a distinct identity. Discard stale packets after reconnect or travel. Cancel active rounds when a player disconnects, and recover through an explicit ready/start flow. Test on two PCs/accounts: simultaneous shots, the same target hit by both peers, gun switching, range restart, repeated travel and a disconnect near round end. A local simulated-peer test cannot establish real network behaviour under delay. Step-by-step development order 1. Prove connection and identity Use two PCs and separate accounts. Confirm who is host, who is guest and which session each belongs to. Log session identity and protocol version in diagnostics without exposing private IDs in public reports. A successful invitation is a connection milestone. It does not prove that range travel, guns, targets or progression are shared. 2. Build a stable roster Resolve current player states, controller/pawn links and display names. Rebuild those references after travel. Keep local/remote role distinct from list order so sorting the UI does not swap ownership. 3. Share appearance and pose Send bounded state snapshots with sequence and session information. Buffer remote poses for interpolation, reject old generations and reset on teleport. Separate cosmetic pose cadence from reliable gameplay events. A proxy model and nameplate can provide useful presence while the game’s real remote pawn has a different replication path. Document which object actually moves and which object is cosmetic. 4. Move both players through a known range Have the host select an allowlisted destination, require the needed readiness, perform the correct shared travel operation and wait for both arrivals. Reacquire weapons and targets only in the new world. Verify both local usable guns and remote visible guns; either can be missing independently. 5. Add reliable gameplay events Give shots, hits and round actions explicit IDs. Keep an acknowledgement/deduplication policy so a retry is safe. Bound the queue and define what happens when it is full rather than endlessly allocating memory or silently losing scoring events. 6. Make one accepted result authoritative For a host-authoritative challenge, validate guest events against the current world/round and known target registry, then publish the accepted state. Cosmetic impact playback should not invoke the game’s reward path again. Validate asset identifiers against a narrow known set. Never interpret a received string as arbitrary Lua, a filesystem path to overwrite or a map to travel to. 7. Handle interruption before adding more modes Disconnect, reconnect, host travel and save transfer can interrupt a round. Retire old session/world generations, explain the cancellation, and clear stale pending actions. A reconnect should not replay last session’s hit queue into a new round. 8. Test the real peer path Repeat simultaneous firing, same-target hits, gun switching, target motion, restart and several travel cycles. Add delay and loss experiments when possible, but keep them distinct from the actual internet session result. Two windows in one process or a simulated peer are not two independent clients. Fields worth designing explicitly Field Purpose Protocol version Reject incompatible packet formats. Session / peer generation Discard an old connection’s data. World / travel generation Avoid applying a previous range’s objects. Round ID Keep events attached to the correct challenge. Sequence / acknowledgement Order replaceable state and retire reliable events. Event ID / target ID Deduplicate actions and resolve game entities without remote pointers. Keep save sharing separate Live gameplay replication and host-save snapshot transfer have different consent, integrity and recovery requirements. The save flow should pause conflicting travel/actions and require a separate apply confirmation. Do not make joining a lobby silently overwrite a guest’s progression. Write a release claim that matches the test “Both players connected and saw models” is a useful result. It is not evidence that both could fire simultaneously, score consistently under delay or restore saves after disconnect. Publish the supported scope and the checks performed, and keep unverified later scenarios distinct from earlier user-confirmed connection. What was checked Local transport fixtures, actor rendering and native gameplay checks exist; the user previously confirmed basic two-PC connection. Later simultaneous shooting, save application and delay-fairness scenarios were not verified in that recorded pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"paths","title":"Game paths & classes","text":"Game paths & classes Useful locations and names observed in the Windows demo. Build-specific reference These paths were checked locally on Steam demo build 26083142 . They are a starting point for discovery, not a promise that another release has the same objects. File locations Location Purpose Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ Shipping executable and loader entry point. Win64/ue4ss/Mods/ Mod folders in the tested loader layout. Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log Loader and Lua output; paths here start at Binaries. %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/ Player saves. Keep these out of mod releases. Ballistic/Content/Paks/ Cooked game content archives. Workshop functions The attachment point widget uses this class path: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C Function Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup. OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup creation. SetupAttachment Fitting or removing an attachment. The workshop HUD is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is named WeaponEditMOde ; preserve that spelling when looking it up. Weapon classes /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart The Blueprint weapon base exposes UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . In the inspected build it has nine boolean inputs. Check the full signature in your own dump before calling it. Maps Name Observed use Bunker_Main Bunker and workshop. Range_SwimmingPool Azure Swimming Pool range. The confirmed pool package is /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool . A range row or menu label alone does not prove that its associated map is included in the demo. For the behaviour behind these names, see Weapon assemblies , Attachments & rails , Shots & ballistics and Range menus & travel . Check your build Use the loader’s object viewer or dump to confirm paths. Keep a small text record of the build, object name, signature and action you tested. See Debugging for a repeatable check. Walkthrough: turn a path into a usable reference 1. Copy the full reflected name A dump entry can include a type prefix, an object path and a member suffix. Preserve the exact case and spelling first. Only remove a type prefix where the lookup API expects the object/function name without it. Do not copy a runtime address from an old dump into a script. Addresses belong to a particular process and can change after garbage collection, travel or a restart. 2. Separate class, object and package names A map package such as /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool is not the same identifier as a generated widget class such as Attach_POI_C. A display label such as Azure Swimming Pool is not either of those paths. Keep fields named mapId, packagePath and displayName distinct in your own data structures. 3. Use context to select an instance Load or locate a class, then find the live object used by the current menu or range. Record its world and full name. If the player switches weapons, the same class can produce a different instance; the feature needs the new instance. 4. Check the member’s signature Before calling a function, read its input/out/return fields in the dump. Before writing a property, record its type and owner. A generated struct field with a long suffix should be copied as a complete identifier, not reconstructed from its display label. Useful research sheets Keep a small per-build sheet for each subsystem: exact class path, observed instance context, relevant functions, parameter types and one successful action. Link that sheet to the guide that explains behaviour; an unannotated catalog alone does not tell a modder what is safe to call. Use Reading data tables for range rows, Weapon assemblies for equipped actors, Workshop UI for editor ownership and Shots & ballistics for hit attribution."},{"id":"custom-content","title":"Custom content","text":"Custom content Know which changes can reuse game assets and which need an asset pipeline. Lua-only mods Lua is useful for changing behaviour, creating UI and assembling objects from assets already cooked into the game. You can prototype layouts by spawning existing meshes and components in the current world. That does not create a new cooked map. The scene still belongs to the existing world, with its lighting, streaming and lifecycle. New models and textures An external .fbx file is a source model, not a game-ready Unreal asset. A Lua script cannot simply use an FBX path as a normal skeletal mesh reference. Proper mesh, material and animation integration generally needs compatible cooked assets or a dedicated runtime importer. This wiki does not provide an importer. Opening the game in Unreal The installed game contains cooked content, rather than the developer’s editable source project. Installing Unreal Editor does not restore the original Blueprints, levels or source assets. The local executable reported the UE5.7 release branch. That is useful context when investigating tools, but it does not establish that any specific editor patch will produce compatible content. Testing a runtime scene Check mesh and material availability in the actual game build. Test lighting and exposure in the world where you spawn objects. Verify collision and a safe player spawn. Keep references to the actors your mod owns. Remove them when the scene closes or the world changes. For a runtime prototype, start with Range menus & travel and Timers & cleanup . For distributing an asset-free Lua mod, see Packaging a Lua mod . Further reading Dmgvol’s UE modding guides cover several Unreal modding approaches. Start with Tools & resources for the loader and archive-inspection references used here. Walkthrough: choose the smallest content pipeline 1. Does the change need a new asset? If the goal is a stat display, a menu action or rearranging existing targets, start with runtime scripting and existing cooked assets. You avoid a cooking pipeline and can verify the behaviour before investing in new art. If the goal is a genuinely new weapon model with correct materials, collision and animation, list those requirements explicitly. A replacement mesh preview satisfies only one part of that list. 2. Inventory available assets Use a browser such as FModel to look for relevant classes, meshes, materials and sounds. Then verify a small set can actually load in the game. A thumbnail or menu row is evidence of a reference, not proof the full asset and its dependencies are usable. 3. Build a runtime prototype Spawn one object, confirm its transform, mobility and visibility, then add collision and interaction. Keep a handle to it for cleanup. Expand to a small layout only after the one-object case is visibly correct. For procedural geometry, the local records established that the procedural mesh component API could create a triangle and a runtime texture import could load a PNG. Those API checks did not establish a correctly lit, textured and positioned RPG model on a held weapon. Keep the capability claim that narrow. 4. Check lighting in the actual world A detached runtime scene still inherits the hosting world’s rendering environment. If structures render but are very dark, inspect sky/ambient lighting, material response and postprocess settings instead of assuming a view-distance problem. A bright emissive workaround is not equivalent to a complete outdoor lighting setup. 5. Use an asset pipeline when the requirement needs it For cooked content, you need a compatible asset/cooking approach and a way to load the packaged result. The installed game is not the developer’s editable project. An engine branch string is context for compatibility research, not a complete reconstruction recipe. Document the exact toolchain, asset types and load mechanism you actually tested. Don’t tell another modder that a particular editor patch will work based only on a matching major/minor version. Acceptance checklist Test appearance, scale, orientation, materials, collision, interaction, cleanup and travel separately. For a weapon, add handling, ammunition, firing and persistence. For a range, add spawn safety, targets, scoring and return flow. Decide which of those are actually in your prototype’s scope before calling it a finished mod."},{"id":"packaging","title":"Packaging a Lua mod","text":"Packaging a Lua mod Make the archive easy to install and leave your development setup out of it. Pick an archive root For an archive intended to be extracted into ue4ss/Mods , include the mod folder itself: MyMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua └── feature.lua README.md Another valid approach is to include the full Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/MyMod path and instruct the user to extract into the game folder. Choose one root and describe it exactly. Mixing the instructions produces doubled or missing folders. Include only release files Use an explicit file list in the build script instead of zipping the whole project. Include the Lua files the mod loads, its enable marker, required mod-owned binaries and the player instructions. Exclude saves, backup saves, captured screenshots, object dumps, runtime reports, logs, tests, browser profiles and unrelated mods. Don’t package the game or the entire loader with a small Lua mod. Version the whole mod Put a version in the startup log and give the archive the same version. A native bridge and its Lua scripts can depend on each other, so ship a complete matching release rather than telling users to update just one file. If a protocol changes, check the peer’s protocol version before accepting gameplay messages. An application version label alone does not prove wire compatibility. Write installation instructions Name the loader requirement and tested game build. Show the exact extraction destination and final folder layout. List controls and configuration file locations. Explain the full-restart requirement used by your build. Explain how to disable the mod and what persisted changes remain. Removing a compatibility script does not necessarily undo an assembly already saved by the game. Check the archive Inspect the file list and compare packaged files with the ones you tested. Try extracting into an empty staging folder and reading the instructions as a new user. For an updated release, confirm that renamed or removed modules cannot remain active from the old install. A checksum can identify the exact artifact you tested; it is not a substitute for testing the artifact’s behaviour. Walkthrough: build a reviewable release 1. Stage a clean copy Create an empty staging directory and copy only the explicitly allowed production files into it. Never use the live Mods directory as the staging root. A development install can contain obsolete modules that make the feature work locally while the archive is missing a dependency. 2. Extract as a new user Open the resulting archive and follow your own instructions into another empty directory. Check the final paths rather than only the archive’s name. A mod-root archive should produce WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua under Mods; a game-root archive should produce its full game-relative tree. 3. Compare the tested files List the staged files and compare hashes with the exact source/installed files used for the test. Include any native DLL needed by that release, but exclude the game and unrelated loader binaries. Check that documentation names the same version as the startup message and archive. 4. Test an upgrade Overlay the new release on a copy of an old install and inspect renamed/removed files. If a stale module can remain active, provide a clear replacement instruction or design the loader entry point so only the intended modules are required. 5. Test disabling With the game closed, disable the mod using its documented method and confirm its callbacks stop after restart. Separately document persisted assemblies or progression that disabling does not undo. A disabled script and a restored save are different outcomes. Write useful release notes Describe the user-visible trigger and result: “Clear attachments now stops when the selected weapon changes” is more useful than “refactored state handling”. Separate new behaviour from fixes and explain important migration steps. For this wiki, edit Next update rather than creating a second patch-notes file. The repository’s contribution guide explains how a dated entry is published."},{"id":"compatibility","title":"Checking game updates","text":"Checking game updates Treat an update as a fresh compatibility check, not an automatic reason to change code. Record the build Write down the Steam build ID, executable version, UE4SS version/commit and your mod version. Keep a copy of the previous release so you can distinguish a new game change from a changed script. The local demo records used build 26083142 and a UE5.7 release-branch executable. These are recorded reference points, not a claim that the reader’s installed game is that build. Check the integration surface Area Check Hooks Function exists, class is loaded, signature and callback parameters still match. Properties Field type and generated name still match; don’t reuse an old offset. Assets Expected class, mesh, material and sound actually load. UI Construction, input, layout, close/reopen and duplicate prevention. Gameplay Equip, fire, hit, switch weapon, restart and travel. Saves Read-only checks first; controlled write/restore tests on a disposable profile. Test in stages Launch the loader without your feature enabled. Enable the mod and confirm clean startup. Trigger one relevant callback and inspect its arguments. Use the actual menu or gameplay feature. Repeat operations that rebuild objects or travel worlds. Run separate multi-PC tests for a multiplayer feature. Stop changing code once a check passes unless there is a new failure or unresolved concern. A large refactor during a compatibility audit makes the result harder to interpret. Record what passed Keep test descriptions specific: “registered eight hooks” is different from “both peers fired and hit the same target”. Record the test method and its limits along with the result. The recorded October demo update passed local menu, travel, model and synthetic gameplay checks. The real two-PC scenarios still had a narrower verified scope. Those two facts can coexist. Report a regression Include reproduction steps, exact content names, version details, a fresh relevant log excerpt and what changed from the last working build. Remove personal paths and IDs before posting logs publicly. If you cannot reproduce a reported issue, preserve the report and the tested limits. Don’t mark it fixed because an unrelated synthetic case passed. Walkthrough: audit one update without guessing 1. Freeze the comparison Keep the previous tested archive and record its checksum. Save a copy of the known-good mod configuration. Make a note of what the player actually confirmed in the old version; do not retroactively treat every planned feature as tested. 2. Test bootstrap independently Confirm the updated game starts, then the loader starts, then your mod starts. Use distinct log milestones. If the initial loader check fails, changing target-scoring code is not a focused response. 3. Compare the integration schema For every required hook or property, compare the full owner/path and reflected types. Record missing, moved or changed entries. For assets, validate type as well as path; an object loaded under a name is not automatically the class or mesh you expected. 4. Re-run relevant native checks Use small fixtures for binding-sensitive calls such as container conversion, child-actor removal and widget construction. If they pass, keep their limits in the report. A synthetic component test is not proof a full player assembly persists correctly. 5. Repeat the player path Open the actual menu, trigger the feature, switch context, travel and restart as appropriate. A UI rework can preserve a class name but change parent panels, event order or focus behaviour, so schema equality does not end the audit. 6. Decide whether a code change is needed If the relevant checks pass and no specific regression remains, report compatibility instead of inventing a fix. If a check fails, make the smallest supported change and repeat the affected checks. Keep unrelated feature work out of the compatibility patch. Results template Build and loader: Baseline release: Schema differences: Startup result: Native fixture results: Actual menu / gameplay results: Real-peer results, if applicable: Save / backup checks: Remaining limits: Artifact checksum:"},{"id":"coverage","title":"Coverage & testing notes","text":"Coverage & testing notes What the wiki explains, what was exercised, and what still needs research. How to read coverage The README percentage is a rough estimate of game-specific documentation, not a measured fraction of all source code, assets or possible mods. Adding setup pages does not establish that more gameplay systems are fully understood. The useful measure is whether a guide gives you a reliable entry point, an example and a clear limit. The table below makes those limits visible without treating every topic as complete. System coverage Area Covered here Still missing Lua setup & lifecycle Loader layout, hooks, discovery, threading, cleanup and debugging. Every loader version and complete API reference. Weapons & attachments Assembly references, rail nesting, native removal and selected stat fields. Every weapon, assembly rule and save/reload combination. Shots & ammunition Shot, projectile-impact and target callback entry points. Complete cartridge construction and ballistic equations. Targets & scoring Discovery, component motion, valid hits, deduplication and custom round design. All target types, rewards and full progression rules. Workshop UI Native button construction, canvas layout and edit-mode lifecycle. Every menu, controller navigation and all accessibility paths. Ranges Table rows, unlock checks, pool travel and restart workflow. A custom cooked-map pipeline and all full-game destinations. Saves & ownership Known slot/class, read-only loading and container distinctions. Full save schema, reliable owned-only filter and all migration cases. Multiplayer State/event separation, authority, visual proxies and interruption handling. A general-purpose SDK and completed real-peer testing of every feature. Evidence levels Found in a dump: the name or field existed in the inspected build. Native fixture: an operation ran against actual engine objects in an isolated test. Local gameplay check: the feature was exercised in the game’s menu or range. Real peer check: separate PCs/accounts performed the scenario together. These levels are different, not interchangeable. A native fixture does not automatically prove a player’s saved assembly or an internet session works. Known open questions The exact owned-only attachment rule remains unresolved. A reported rail-removal stat penalty was not reproduced by the recorded synthetic fixture. A visible custom range card did not establish a new map, and runtime-scene lighting experiments did not produce a finished custom range. The ballistics , ownership and multiplayer guides explain the relevant boundaries in more detail. Add a finding Record the game build, loader version, exact object/function and steps you tested. A small reproducible result is more useful than a long list of untested classes. Follow the repository’s CONTRIBUTING.md when opening a documentation PR. How to add a documented capability Describe the exact player or modder action the guide is meant to enable. List the required game objects and loader APIs. Mark which names are only observed in a dump and which operations were actually exercised. Provide prerequisites, an example’s required context and an expected observable result. Include at least one failure case and a way to return to a known state. Record the game and loader builds that support the result. A new page can make an existing capability easier to use without expanding the known game API. Conversely, one carefully verified function can add useful coverage even before it warrants a long article. Do not use page count as a substitute for evidence. How the examples are labelled Complete files include their required imports and startup wiring. Context fragments explicitly name variables that must already be valid. Pure Lua patterns operate on ordinary tables and values. Illustrative output shows a format and is not presented as a captured run. The downloadable inspector is educational source adapted from recorded local patterns. Documentation builds, HTML checks and mock tests do not establish that a real engine object or future game build has the same behaviour. The testing guide explains the separate checks. Research priorities The largest remaining gaps include complete cartridge construction and ballistics internals, a reliable ownership rule for every attachment, full save schema/migrations, every weapon/target type and a supported custom cooked-content pipeline. New beginner material helps people start, but those gaps need direct investigation rather than longer generic explanations."},{"id":"tools","title":"Tools & resources","text":"Tools & resources The main references to keep open while you work. UE4SS The loader runs Lua mods and exposes Unreal objects and functions. Match API guidance to your installed version; the development documentation may describe APIs newer than your local loader. Releases and downloads Installation guide Development documentation Lua API overview Game inspection Start with the UE4SS object viewer and object dump to inspect what the current game session has loaded. Use an archive browser when you need to look for cooked content that isn’t loaded yet. FModel — Unreal archive browser. FModel getting started — configuration and usage. Tool support depends on the game’s engine and archive format. Finding an asset in an archive does not guarantee it can be loaded in every game mode. Unreal modding UE_Modding by Dmgvol collects techniques and guides for Unreal games. Use it to research asset-based mods alongside the Lua approach described here. The game Ballistic.exe on Steam This wiki documents local observations and practical examples. It does not ship a game framework, SDK or game assets. Choose a tool by the question Question Start with Expected evidence Did my script load? A fresh UE4SS log. Your unique startup prefix and version. What functions exist now? Object viewer / object dump. Exact loaded names and reflected signatures. Is an asset in the installation? Archive browser. Package presence and dependencies to investigate. Can that asset run here? A small game-thread load/spawn probe. A valid object and a visible/functional result. Did my logic handle duplicates? A pure Lua test or controlled fixture. An asserted state transition. Does this work over Steam? Two real PCs/accounts. Both screens agree under the tested conditions. A practical discovery session Pick one behaviour and reproduce it normally in the game. Locate the relevant class/function in the loaded object view. Copy the exact path and parameter types into a small research note. Install a read-only logging hook after the class is loaded. Trigger the normal behaviour and compare callback arguments. Only then prototype a change using a disposable context. Archive inspection and object inspection answer different questions. Keep both results when they disagree instead of assuming one tool is wrong: the asset may exist without being loaded, or a reference may point at content unavailable in this build. Version drift in documentation The development UE4SS docs can describe features newer than the local loader. Prefer a small known API surface when writing a portable example, and check the installed version before adopting a new delayed-action or mod-management API. Keep links to the primary references with your notes. A copied snippet without its API version and required context is harder to maintain than a short explained example."}];
